43787502d17cd191848cc95\\\\nVerdict: RESIDUALS — NOT MERGE-READY\\\\n\\\\n# Step-7 re-check round 6\\\\n\\\\nThe native-endpoint repair works for all four requested counts, through TWO parsed PHP\\\\nreceptions with the first result live. The float-count disposition and the two count tests'\\\\nassertions are accepted. However, the doubling probe creates a new admission endpoint:\\\\nan admitted count immediately below that endpoint becomes ForeignException on its next\\\\nordinary reception. This is the same identity defect as re-check 5, at a different threshold.\\\\nOne P2 residual remains.\\\\n\\\\nSource, tests, fixtures, configuration and git history were reviewed without modification.\\\\nOnly this requested findings artifact was written.\\\\n\\\\n## P2 — doubling creates another endpoint that ordinary acquisition crosses\\\\n\\\\nLocation: src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2956-2958, specifically the doubling predicate\\\\nat :2958. Consumer: php.rt/receive-condition at :2995-2996.\\\\nCoverage gap: test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj:628-631 and :711 cover native endpoints\\\\nand an already-over-limit count, but not the new admission boundary.\\\\n\\\\nLet B be the composition's ::integer/max-bits, and T = 2^(B-1). For exact integer counts,\\\\nthe doubling test admits |rc| < T. It therefore admits rc = T-1:\\\\n\\\\n- rc+1 = T is an integer under the module limit (B bits).\\\\n- rc+rc = 2^B-2 is also an integer under that limit (B bits).\\\\n- Reception and ordinary PHP ownership produce rc = T.\\\\n- At the next reception, rc+1 still succeeds, and rc-1 still succeeds.\\\\n- But rc+rc = 2^B now returns ::integer/bit-limit, so obj-val? rejects the same local\\\\n  Throwable and receive-condition wraps it as ForeignException.\\\\n\\\\nThe failed doubling is a computation introduced solely by classification. No ownership\\\\noperation in this reproduction needs that doubled value. This is not genuinely unsupported\\\\ncount state: the runtime just produced it from an admitted count, and both ownership\\\\noperations still work.\\\\n\\\\n### Firsthand reproduction at the reviewed tip\\\\n\\\\nThe actual PHP harness installs B=100000. Construct this payload in the guest:\\\\n\\\\n```clojure\\\\n(assoc {} :php/obj\\\\n       (cell/new\\\\n         (assoc {:class \\\\\\\"Exception\\\\\\\"}\\\\n                :rc (integer/sub (integer/shift-left 1 99999) 1))))\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nEvery operation constructing that count is an admitted integer-module operation and stays\\\\ninside its configured limit. No raw-host arithmetic or over-limit intermediate is needed.\\\\n\\\\nSupply a thrown condition containing the payload through a callable's actual\\\\n:yang.call/raise exit. Invoke this parsed/lowered source receiver twice, retaining the\\\\nfirst returned value in a guest binding until after the second call:\\\\n\\\\n```php\\\\nfunction receive($f) {\\\\n    try { return $f(); }\\\\n    catch (Throwable $e) { return $e; }\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nNo count edits, guest numeric operations, or condition changes occur between receptions.\\\\nObserved through current worktree source, production host registry and the JVM AST walker,\\\\nwith normal completion and process exit 0:\\\\n\\\\n| Initial count | Initially admitted | First identity / class | Admitted after first return | Second identity / class |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| 1 | true | true / Exception | true | true / Exception |\\\\n| 9223372036854775806 | true | true / Exception | true | true / Exception |\\\\n| 9223372036854775807 | true | true / Exception | true | true / Exception |\\\\n| -9223372036854775808 | true | true / Exception | true | true / Exception |\\\\n| 9007199254740992 | true | true / Exception | true | true / Exception |\\\\n| 2^99999-1 | true | true / Exception | **false** | **false / ForeignException** |\\\\n| 2^99999 | false | false / ForeignException | false | false / ForeignException |\\\\n| 2^100000 | false | false / ForeignException | false | false / ForeignException |\\\\n\\\\nFor the failing T-1 row, the count after the first return has 100000 magnitude bits,\\\\nand BOTH integer/add(count,1) and integer/sub(count,1) return exact integers.\\\\nThe second result retains the complete original condition, including\\\\n{:complete [:evidence]}, and the original payload. Retention works; classification\\\\nand identity are wrong.\\\\n\\\\nAn independent integer-module diagnostic at the profile floor B=4096 likewise observed:\\\\n\\\\n```clojure\\\\n{:before-double-integer true\\\\n :after-double :yin.vm.integer/bit-limit\\\\n :after-increment-integer true\\\\n :after-decrement-integer true}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n### Ruling on probe soundness\\\\n\\\\nThe probe is sound as an immediate arithmetic safety margin: for B>=4096, passing doubling\\\\nplaces |rc| strictly below T, so subsequent individual +1 and -1 operations have ample room\\\\nbefore the ACTUAL arithmetic limit 2^B-1. I found no exact-integer carrier bypass that passes\\\\nboth probes and immediately installs ::bit-limit during an ordinary acquisition/release.\\\\nThe preserved exact domain predicate still excludes ratios and float content before\\\\narithmetic, and both probes are pure.\\\\n\\\\nThat does NOT make the admission policy stable under ordinary ownership. The comments'\\\\nhuge number of acquisitions concerns reaching the arithmetic limit, while reaching the\\\\npredicate's own exclusion needs ONE acquisition from the legally constructed T-1 count.\\\\nThus \\\\\\\"crossing the limit unreachable\\\\\\\" does not establish local identity, and \\\\\\\"No count the\\\\nrepaired arithmetic itself produces is ever refused\\\\\\\" is false. Nor is \\\\\\\"every still-operable\\\\ncount is admitted\\\\\\\" true: T is directly constructible by integer/shift-left and supports\\\\nboth ownership operations, but is rejected.\\\\n\\\\nRequired completion:\\\\n\\\\n- Preserve the native-endpoint repair, integer/integer?, and exact integer/add/sub.\\\\n- Repair identity-stable reception across ordinary ownership changes, including T-1 to T.\\\\n  Address the ownership limit policy consistently; merely moving a finite admission cutoff\\\\n  or probing one extra acquisition reproduces the same issue at the new cutoff.\\\\n- Add this T-1 two-reception case to the source regression, asserting original identity and\\\\n  Exception class on both results with the first result live.\\\\n- Keep the truly over-limit full-retention control, and correct the stronger lifetime and\\\\n  operability claims in the source comments and engineering rationale.\\\\n- Do not apply a bare deletion that lets ::bit-limit become stored :rc, and do not introduce\\\\n  a broad host catch.\\\\n\\\\nThis follows the same §8.10.7 rule 1 / owner v2 R2 ruling as re-check 5. The counterexample\\\\nis an actual runtime-produced state from a count this implementation itself admitted,\\\\nnot a demand to recognize arbitrary corrupted state.\\\\n\\\\n## Ruling 2 — float-count choice accepted\\\\n\\\\ndata/float64 1 preserves float content: a JVM/Dart double and a Float64 carrier on JS.\\\\ndao.host.integer/exact-integer? rejects those representations on all three hosts.\\\\nThat is the appropriate portable foreign-count pin.\\\\n\\\\ndata/float-value 1 deliberately unwraps to a host arithmetic number: a Double on JVM,\\\\na double on Dart, but a bare Number on JS. The JS exact-integer predicate admits safe\\\\nintegral Numbers and cannot distinguish that value from integer 1. Omitting a uniform\\\\nforeign-disposition assertion for this host-divergent operation is accepted. This removes\\\\nno existing committed pin: float-count is additive in this range. The JVM-only diagnostic\\\\nin re-check 5 did not establish a portable rejection requirement for float-value.\\\\n\\\\nSources inspected: yin/vm/data.cljc:431-453, dao/jing/cbor.cljc:212-248,\\\\ndao/host/integer.cljc:66-82.\\\\n\\\\n## Ruling 3 — count-test assertions accepted, boundary coverage incomplete\\\\n\\\\nphp-count-acquisition-test (:611-685):\\\\n\\\\n- Four counts, six boolean observations per count, 24 expected keys per backend.\\\\n- Each shape uses the SAME injected callable and condition twice. The first value is\\\\n  referenced in the final observation after the second reception, so it remains live.\\\\n- Both return routes, both original handle identities, and both exact Exception classes\\\\n  are asserted. The PHP-side get_class comparison distinguishes Exception from a generated\\\\n  ForeignException before rendering; equality compares the original cell-bearing handle.\\\\n- The class-observation calls use ordinary PHP argument acquisition/release. Nothing in\\\\n  the observation manually resets the count between receptions.\\\\n- The new class checks strengthen the old route/identity checks. A local delivery does not\\\\n  retain a foreign condition, so absence of a condition-retention comparison on these\\\\n  LOCAL cases is appropriate.\\\\n- Missing coverage is the probe's own endpoint above, not an incorrect expected value.\\\\n\\\\nphp-count-limit-test (:688-741):\\\\n\\\\n- Its actual construction uses raw host + on TWO BigInt values seeded by\\\\n  integer/shift-left: 2^99999 + 2^99999 = 2^100000. It does not use raw host *.\\\\n  Both seeds fit the harness's explicit 100000-bit limit; their sum exceeds it.\\\\n  This avoids the Long-overflow problem previously identified for unseeded raw arithmetic.\\\\n- The conformance registry uses the TypeScript linked harness's integer limits, explicitly\\\\n  100000 bits. The count's lane-specific width is documented.\\\\n- The test asserts a returned wrapper, PHP ForeignException classification, complete\\\\n  retained-condition equality including evidence, and original retained-payload identity\\\\n  through a later activation. All three backend keys are required.\\\\n- These expectations are correct. The test exercises the first increment probe's rejection;\\\\n  it cannot establish the doubling predicate's stability or necessity.\\\\n- My parsed-source over-limit control independently observed ForeignException and full\\\\n  condition/payload retention. The linked three-backend test results are supplied gate\\\\n  evidence, not a rerun claimed here.\\\\n\\\\nThe 2da48669 repair adds exactly the missing closer ending php-count-acquisition-test.\\\\nA Clojure reader check at the tip confirms both count tests are separate top-level deftests.\\\\n\\\\n## Ruling 4 — pins and scope\\\\n\\\\nExactly three files change in 4a246994..2da48669: the PHP prelude, PHP exception test, and\\\\nr11 engineering findings. The only executable prelude change in this range is the count\\\\nadmission expression; the addref hunk changes comments. addref/delref arithmetic bodies,\\\\ninteger module, lowerer, finalize!, host-names, PHP exports, aliases, receiver/mint privacy\\\\nand reserved provenance doors are unchanged.\\\\n\\\\nThe two endpoint payload constructions move from foreign-disposition coverage to LOCAL\\\\ncoverage under the explicit re-check-5 ruling; they are not deleted. Near-max and 2^53\\\\ncases remain and gain the second reception and class assertions. The eleven-shape foreign\\\\nmatrix retains malformed, competing-marker, bad-class, absent/non-number and ratio cases,\\\\nand adds float content. Its four full-retention observations remain (44 keys/backend).\\\\nOther fixtures, shared kit expectations, nil pins and directed legs are unchanged.\\\\nNo unrelated pin is weakened or scope widened.\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence and gate disposition\\\\n\\\\nFirsthand this round:\\\\n\\\\n- Full requested diff, architecture rules, §8.10.7, owner ruling v2, re-check 5 and r11\\\\n  rationale inspected, plus integer, host-carrier, data/float, ownership and lowering paths.\\\\n- Parsed/lowered source diagnostic above: eight count controls, normal VM completion,\\\\n  process exit 0. No source parsing diagnostics.\\\\n- Independent profile-floor integer diagnostic: headroom transition reproduced at B=4096.\\\\n- Static retention-surface/adoption/mint-reachability pins: 3 tests, 14 passes, 0F/0E.\\\\n- Clojure reader verifies separate top-level count tests.\\\\n- git diff --check 4a246994..2da48669 passed; tracked working-tree status stayed clean.\\\\n\\\\nDiagnostics used current worktree source/test roots first and only existing cached jar\\\\ndependencies and generated parser class directories from the phpexc checkout. The PHP\\\\nmanifest.edn and PhpParser.g4 were checked byte-identical to that checkout. No builds,\\\\ndependency resolution, parser generation or test-cache writes were performed. Shell\\\\nlaunchers emitted read-only temporary-cache warnings, but the reported JVM diagnostics\\\\ncompleted successfully. A preliminary diagnostic launch used a nonexistent Python path;\\\\nthe successful results above come from the corrected invocation.\\\\n\\\\nThe dispatch's focused jupiter tip evidence (15 selected pins, three disclosed lane-block\\\\nfailures only; family 58 tests/725 assertions green; kondo/cljstyle clean) is accepted AS\\\\nSUPPLIED. Those tests do not include T-1. I did not rerun the linked batches, full directed\\\\nmatrix, Node/Dart boundary harnesses, contract acceptance kit or foundation gate.\\\\nThe three :php/local-reception :payload-identical lane-blocked failures retain their prior\\\\ndisposition. Existing nil coverage notes, context/suspension lifetime obligations and\\\\nsteward-owned remote/foundation gates remain as previously recorded.\\\\n\\\\n**RESIDUALS — NOT MERGE-READY.** The requested native-endpoint scenarios are repaired.\\\\nThe new doubling endpoint must also preserve local class and identity after ordinary\\\\nacquisition before this slice closes.\\\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-12 23:57:01 GMT\\\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-13 06:57:01 +0700 (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh)\\\\nCoding-Agent: codex (gpt-6.1-sol)\\\\nSession-ID: unavailable in this environment\\\\nRole: Architect, read-only step-7 re-check round 5\\\\nReviewed-Base: 265c4a4590259f2ea592cb81cd6991aa7b8afde8\\\\nReviewed-Tip: 59329a054a66041cb2c5816aae0e97dc65e2b542\\\\nVerdict: RESIDUALS — NOT MERGE-READY\\\\n\\\\n# Step-7 re-check round 5\\\\n\\\\nThe exact-integer repair closes both concrete count-domain defects from re-check 4:\\\\nordinary acquisition no longer uses native overflowing/mixed-carrier arithmetic, and a\\\\ncomputed Ratio is rejected before integer/compare. However, I rule AGAINST the restored\\\\nendpoint disposition. One P2 residual remains: a value admitted and returned as the\\\\noriginal local Exception becomes ForeignException on its next ordinary reception because\\\\nthe first reception moved its count onto the excluded maximum. This was reproduced\\\\nthrough parsed/lowered PHP source at the reviewed tip.\\\\n\\\\nThe source and tests were reviewed read-only. Only this requested findings artifact was\\\\nwritten; no source, tests, fixtures, configuration, caches, or git history were modified.\\\\n\\\\n## P2 residual — ordinary ownership changes turn a local exception into a foreign one\\\\n\\\\nLocation: src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2937-2939.\\\\nConsumer: php.rt/receive-condition at :2975-2977.\\\\nCoverage gap: test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj:627-644 exercises only one reception.\\\\n\\\\nThe test's own near-max payload is sufficient:\\\\n\\\\n```clojure\\\\n(assoc {} :php/obj\\\\n       (cell/new (assoc {:class \\\\\\\"Exception\\\\\\\"}\\\\n                       :rc (integer/parse \\\\\\\"9223372036854775806\\\\\\\"))))\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nSupply its thrown condition through a callable's actual :yang.call/raise exit, and call\\\\nthis ordinary PHP receiver TWICE with the same callable/condition:\\\\n\\\\n```php\\\\nfunction receive($f) {\\\\n    try { return $f(); }\\\\n    catch (Throwable $e) { return $e; }\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nKeep the first returned value live while making the second call. No count edits, numeric\\\\noperations, or malformed-condition tricks occur between the two calls. Firsthand results\\\\nfrom current-tip source, production host registry and JVM AST walker:\\\\n\\\\n| Initial count | First result identical | Count after first return | Second result identical | Second class |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| 1 | true | 2 | true | Exception |\\\\n| 9223372036854775806 | true | 9223372036854775807 | **false** | **ForeignException** |\\\\n| 9007199254740992 | true | 9007199254740993 | true | Exception |\\\\n\\\\nThe failing case completed normally at the VM level; this is a semantic identity failure,\\\\nnot another host overflow. Its count after the second call remains 9223372036854775807.\\\\nA separate parserless two-reception probe gave the same classification failure.\\\\n\\\\nThe first call proves exactly why the engineer's claim that the endpoint is \\\\\\\"not a count\\\\nphp's machinery could have produced\\\\\\\" is false: php's own repaired ownership operations\\\\njust produced it. The value is still throwable by php/throw-value, which checks the\\\\nThrowable hierarchy and uses the repaired exact addref. Only boundary reception changes\\\\nits class/identity. An ordinary catch retaining one more reference cannot make an otherwise\\\\nunchanged local exception foreign.\\\\n\\\\n### Endpoint ruling\\\\n\\\\nSection 8.10.7 rule 1 and the owner ruling v2 R2 require already-local exceptions to retain\\\\nordinary catching and identity. Rule 3 applies to an OTHERWISE-foreign payload; it does\\\\nnot authorize reclassifying this runtime-produced state just because its private reference\\\\ncount reached the source-language integer endpoint.\\\\n\\\\nThe accepted Python implementation at prelude.cljc:1067-1079 recognizes its own exception\\\\nrepresentation/hierarchy and raises that value unchanged. It has no PHP reference-count\\\\nanalogue that would justify a signed-64-bit cutoff on PHP's now-exact ownership arithmetic.\\\\nPHP still needs representation and operability checks; that does not make every previous\\\\ncount cutoff a semantic invariant after its original arithmetic reason has been removed.\\\\n\\\\nI therefore rule the other way on the handover question: the signed-64-bit endpoints,\\\\nby themselves, are not a reason to wrap an otherwise admitted Throwable under the repaired\\\\narithmetic. The positive maximum counterexample alone is enough to reject the restored\\\\npolicy. The minimum-only exclusion likewise lacks the old decrement-overflow justification;\\\\nany separately proposed count invariant must be justified consistently, not inferred from\\\\nthe old host carrier limits.\\\\n\\\\nRequired completion:\\\\n\\\\n- Preserve exact integer/add and integer/sub and the exact-integer admission predicate.\\\\n- Make reception preserve local type/identity across ordinary acquisitions and later\\\\n  receptions, including the runtime-produced endpoint and beyond-native results.\\\\n- Extend the near-max source pin to two calls while the first result remains live, with\\\\n  original identity and original Exception class on BOTH results.\\\\n- Keep endpoint coverage, but revise its asserted disposition under this ruling instead\\\\n  of deleting the cases. Keep malformed, competing-marker, ratio, missing/non-number and\\\\n  legitimate-local controls with complete-condition/evidence/payload checks for foreign\\\\n  cases.\\\\n\\\\nThis revises the old endpoint disposition explicitly in response to the requested ruling;\\\\nit is not permission to silently invert or remove unrelated pins.\\\\n\\\\n### Do not apply the documented bare revert without a limit-aware replacement\\\\n\\\\nThe engineer is correct about the separate integer-module limit hazard: integer/integer?\\\\nrecognizes carriers without checking ::integer/max-bits; integer/add and integer/sub can\\\\nreturn ::bit-limit. Installing that keyword as :rc would make the next integer operation\\\\nrefuse. Merely deleting both bounds and accepting EVERY exact integer is not a complete fix.\\\\n\\\\nThat hazard does not justify the particular strict 64-bit boundary: MAX, MAX+1 and MIN are\\\\nwell inside the profile's minimum 4096-bit arithmetic limit. Count validation/limit handling\\\\nmust distinguish genuinely unsupported state from the local results ordinary ownership\\\\nnow produces. No broad host catch is authorized.\\\\n\\\\nTwo details in the findings' supporting argument should also be corrected:\\\\n(1) raw clojure.core/* on two Long operands does not auto-promote past overflow; a BigInt\\\\nseed is needed for the proposed repeated-squaring construction; (2) an initial 63-bit\\\\nbound does not prove ALL subsequent ownership results stay 64-bit. Neither correction\\\\nnegates the real ::bit-limit hazard, and neither is the blocking reproduction above.\\\\n\\\\n## Ruling 1 — both re-check-4 count-domain counterexamples are repaired\\\\n\\\\nphp/addref (:1856-1857) and php/delref (:1869) now use the integer module. Its checked big\\\\npath promotes operands to the host big carrier, computes exactly, and demotes to the\\\\ncanonical carrier; its fast path falls back when the safe native result range is exceeded.\\\\nThe ordinary lower-return acquisition still calls php/addref (lower.cljc:2945), so the\\\\nrepair applies after reception as required, including copying/rethrow operations using it.\\\\n\\\\nThe source receiver above now completes its FIRST near-max call with original identity;\\\\nthe previous native-long overflow is closed. The 2^53 decimal also completes with original\\\\nidentity through both calls on the JVM.\\\\n\\\\nFor JS, dao.host.integer/exact-integer? admits BigInt, promote keeps both arithmetic\\\\noperands in BigInt form, and demote produces the canonical Number/BigInt result. Thus the\\\\ncount is no longer sent to raw BigInt-plus-Number arithmetic. integer/sub demotes zero to\\\\nthe native zero, preserving the existing (= n 0) destruction test. This is verified by\\\\nreading the actual host/module implementations; no complete Node/Dart runtime green is\\\\nclaimed by this review. The decimal in the JVM pin is a Long and alone would not prove JS.\\\\n\\\\nAt :2936 integer/integer? now precedes BOTH integer/compare calls. Its implementation\\\\ndelegates directly to host/exact-integer?, so a computed JVM Ratio, float64 content, a\\\\nDouble, nil, or a non-number never reaches those comparisons. The old representation\\\\nclassifier php/int? and the broad data/number? check are gone from count admission.\\\\n\\\\nFirsthand direct reception controls, including full evidence and payload identity:\\\\n\\\\n| Count expression | ForeignException | Original local identity | Whole condition retained | Original payload retained |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| (/ 1 2) | true | false | true | true |\\\\n| (data/float64 1) | true | false | true | true |\\\\n| (data/float-value 1) | true | false | true | true |\\\\n| (/ 6 3) | false | true | n/a | n/a |\\\\n| parse MAX | true | false | true | true |\\\\n| parse MIN | true | false | true | true |\\\\n\\\\nThe last two rows record CURRENT behavior, which the endpoint ruling above rejects;\\\\nthe retention machinery itself works on those rejected paths. The computed-ratio\\\\nresidual is CLOSED, not merely hidden behind another refusal.\\\\n\\\\n## Ruling 3 — accept leaving finalize!'s raw unpin in this round\\\\n\\\\nAt prelude.cljc:4382 finalize! overwrites :rc with the literal native 1 BEFORE invoking\\\\nthe destructor. The raw subtraction at :4386 therefore does not consume the large count\\\\nadmitted from a boundary. Under ordinary PHP ownership, only acquisitions/releases made\\\\nduring this destructor contribute to its unpin value.\\\\n\\\\nI accept that scoped argument for the requested residual: the near-max and JS-BigInt\\\\nreceived-carrier counterexamples cannot flow into this subtraction. No conversion is\\\\nrequired for this review. This is not a proof against arbitrary guest cell/set! corruption\\\\nduring a destructor, nor an unrestricted totality claim for an unbounded number of\\\\nacquisitions. Neither is the counterexample established here.\\\\n\\\\n## Ruling 4 — pins, publication surface and scope\\\\n\\\\nThe requested range is one commit and exactly three changed files: the PHP prelude,\\\\nthe PHP exception test, and the r10 findings. No other frontend, VM implementation,\\\\nlowerer, shared kit, fixture, ledger, or design file changes in this range.\\\\n\\\\nNo previous assertion or payload case is removed. The two original endpoint payload\\\\nforms remain, with their foreign-retention observations; the computed-ratio case and\\\\nthe ordinary catch/return acquisition test are additive. The committed shape matrix is\\\\nnow TWELVE cases with FOUR observations each (48 expected keys per backend), rather than\\\\nthe supplied gate summary's 45-key description. I did not inspect that remote batch's\\\\nraw output and do not equate these cardinalities; the direct ratio result above is\\\\nfirsthand evidence of the repaired ratio path.\\\\n\\\\nAdding integer/integer? to host-names declares an already-existing integer module export,\\\\nnot a new PHP publication door. Removing data/number? removes an unused declaration.\\\\nThe PHP exports, aliases, reserved provenance doors, receiver/mint privacy and mint call\\\\ngraph are unchanged. There is no broad host catch or arbitrary-condition mint helper.\\\\n\\\\naddref/delref are outside the original receiver's textual region, but changing ownership\\\\noperations is expressly required by re-check 4's whole-consumption-path residual and\\\\nthis dispatch. No unrelated ownership algorithm changed. The literal surface-registry\\\\nfile 1794343000000 remains absent here, as disclosed in the original review; scope is\\\\nchecked against the available binding briefs and the actual diff, not a nonexistent\\\\nregistry artifact.\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence and remaining gate disposition\\\\n\\\\nFirsthand this round:\\\\n\\\\n- Full requested source/test diff, re-check 4, r10 findings, governing architecture,\\\\n  §8.10.7, owner ruling v2, accepted Python reception and relevant integer/ownership/\\\\n  lowering implementations inspected.\\\\n- Bounded current-source JVM diagnostics completed with exit 0: direct count controls\\\\n  and the parsed/lowered receiver called twice with three count controls.\\\\n- Static surface/adoption/reachability pins: 3 tests, 14 passes, 0 failures, 0 errors.\\\\n- git diff --check 265c4a45..59329a05 passed; tracked working-tree status remained clean.\\\\n\\\\nThe source probe borrowed only existing generated parser class directories from the\\\\nphpexc checkout, plus cached dependency jars; current worktree source/test roots led\\\\nthe classpath. PHP manifest.edn and PhpParser.g4 match that checkout byte-for-byte.\\\\nNo builds, generated files, dependency resolution or cache writes were performed.\\\\nA preliminary source-probe invocation had a diagnostic-script read error; the\\\\nreported source table is from the corrected, successful bounded invocation, not that\\\\nattempt. The parserless identity failure was independently observed as well.\\\\n\\\\nThe orchestrator's tip gate evidence in the dispatch is accepted as supplied evidence:\\\\nfocused count/nil/shape/directed/adoption/reachability batches, listed namespaces, kondo\\\\nand cljstyle. Those successes cover the existing assertions; a one-reception count pin\\\\ndoes not exercise the newly demonstrated second reception. I did not rerun the linked\\\\nbatches, contract kit, Node/Dart boundary harness, full directed matrix or foundation gate.\\\\n\\\\nThe three disclosed :php/local-reception :payload-identical lane-blocked failures retain\\\\ntheir previous disposition; no changed shared fixture/expectation contributes to the\\\\nnew failure. Existing nil-pin class/provenance coverage notes, suspension/context lifetime\\\\nand steward-owned remote/foundation obligations remain as previously recorded.\\\\n\\\\n**RESIDUALS — NOT MERGE-READY.** The arithmetic and computed-ratio repairs stand.\\\\nComplete stable local identity at subsequent reception before this PHP step-7 slice closes.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/exception-track/1794538889000-architect-step7-recheck7.prompt.md:6:# Task: step-7 re-check round 7 — the r12 doubling-probe removal (e80e1404)\\ncollab/exception-track/1794538889000-architect-step7-recheck7.prompt.md:9:required completion prescribed: the doubling predicate is REMOVED from php/obj-val?'s count\\ncollab/exception-track/1794538889000-architect-step7-recheck7.prompt.md:11:yield an integer under the composition's limits); T-1 and the interval [T, limit) counts are\\ncollab/exception-track/1794538889000-architect-step7-recheck7.prompt.md:12:admitted LOCAL; the over-limit 2^100000 shape stays FOREIGN retained whole; finalize! and\\ncollab/exception-track/1794538889000-architect-step7-recheck7.prompt.md:16:- BEFORE (parent prelude 2da48669 + r12 test file): php-count-acquisition-test 3 failures\\ncollab/exception-track/1794538889000-architect-step7-recheck7.prompt.md:19:- AFTER (e80e1404): batch1 (count-acquisition, count-limit, handle-shapes, nil-condition,\\ncollab/exception-track/1794538889000-architect-step7-recheck7.prompt.md:28:consequence of admitting [T, limit): the increment probe alone means a count admitted at\\ncollab/exception-track/1794538889000-architect-step7-recheck7.prompt.md:29:T+K operates until its increment hits the module limit — is any reception path observable\\ncollab/exception-track/1794538889000-architect-step7-recheck7.prompt.md:30:difference left vs pure domain membership, and is that acceptable per §8.10.7? (3) anything\\ncollab/exception-track/1794532719000-architect-step7-recheck6.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md:12:The native-endpoint repair works for all four requested counts, through TWO parsed PHP\\ncollab/exception-track/1794532719000-architect-