ission leaves a **release**-side path that stores `::bit-limit`\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:183:as `:rc` — the very thing re-check6 forbids (\\\"Do not apply a bare deletion that lets `::bit-limit`\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:185:both probes and immediately installs `::bit-limit` during an ordinary **acquisition/release**\\\").\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:199:→ `::bit-limit` → the keyword is `assoc`'d into the cell as `:rc`, and the next ownership\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:204:This does **not** recreate the doubling's defect, and the distinction is the round's whole lesson:\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:209:to refuse counts at the *negative* ceiling, where release genuinely cannot be performed. It is one\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:225:# expect: php-count-acquisition-test FAILS on t-minus-one-second-class and\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:231:# expect green, with php-count-acquisition-test now reporting 30 keys/backend and\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:232:# php-count-limit-test still 4 keys/backend. Capture verbatim.\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:243:#    increment probe only, and confirm the ::bit-limit keyword lands in :rc and the next\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:251:git commit -m \\\"fix(yang.php): admission probes the increment only — the doubling headroom created its own endpoint (r12, sol's re-check6)\\\"\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:259:- `t-minus-one` mints a ~12.5 KB integer count and the shape runs in the same composed program as\\ncollab/exception-track/1794533165000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r12.qwen3.8-max.findings.md:270:  reason about headroom without inventing a computation, or a saturating count policy), which is\\ncollab/exception-track/1794532719000-architect-step7-recheck6.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:9:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_3\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/exception-track/1794527358000-architect-step7-recheck5.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md collab/exception-track/1794527932000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r11.qwen3.8-max.findings.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-12 23:57:01 GMT\\\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-13 06:57:01 +0700 (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh)\\\\nCoding-Agent: codex (gpt-6.1-sol)\\\\nSession-ID: unavailable in this environment\\\\nRole: Architect, read-only step-7 re-check round 5\\\\nReviewed-Base: 265c4a4590259f2ea592cb81cd6991aa7b8afde8\\\\nReviewed-Tip: 59329a054a66041cb2c5816aae0e97dc65e2b542\\\\nVerdict: RESIDUALS — NOT MERGE-READY\\\\n\\\\n# Step-7 re-check round 5\\\\n\\\\nThe exact-integer repair closes both concrete count-domain defects from re-check 4:\\\\nordinary acquisition no longer uses native overflowing/mixed-carrier arithmetic, and a\\\\ncomputed Ratio is rejected before integer/compare. However, I rule AGAINST the restored\\\\nendpoint disposition. One P2 residual remains: a value admitted and returned as the\\\\noriginal local Exception becomes ForeignException on its next ordinary reception because\\\\nthe first reception moved its count onto the excluded maximum. This was reproduced\\\\nthrough parsed/lowered PHP source at the reviewed tip.\\\\n\\\\nThe source and tests were reviewed read-only. Only this requested findings artifact was\\\\nwritten; no source, tests, fixtures, configuration, caches, or git history were modified.\\\\n\\\\n## P2 residual — ordinary ownership changes turn a local exception into a foreign one\\\\n\\\\nLocation: src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2937-2939.\\\\nConsumer: php.rt/receive-condition at :2975-2977.\\\\nCoverage gap: test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj:627-644 exercises only one reception.\\\\n\\\\nThe test's own near-max payload is sufficient:\\\\n\\\\n```clojure\\\\n(assoc {} :php/obj\\\\n       (cell/new (assoc {:class \\\\\\\"Exception\\\\\\\"}\\\\n                       :rc (integer/parse \\\\\\\"9223372036854775806\\\\\\\"))))\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nSupply its thrown condition through a callable's actual :yang.call/raise exit, and call\\\\nthis ordinary PHP receiver TWICE with the same callable/condition:\\\\n\\\\n```php\\\\nfunction receive($f) {\\\\n    try { return $f(); }\\\\n    catch (Throwable $e) { return $e; }\\\\n}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nKeep the first returned value live while making the second call. No count edits, numeric\\\\noperations, or malformed-condition tricks occur between the two calls. Firsthand results\\\\nfrom current-tip source, production host registry and JVM AST walker:\\\\n\\\\n| Initial count | First result identical | Count after first return | Second result identical | Second class |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| 1 | true | 2 | true | Exception |\\\\n| 9223372036854775806 | true | 9223372036854775807 | **false** | **ForeignException** |\\\\n| 9007199254740992 | true | 9007199254740993 | true | Exception |\\\\n\\\\nThe failing case completed normally at the VM level; this is a semantic identity failure,\\\\nnot another host overflow. Its count after the second call remains 9223372036854775807.\\\\nA separate parserless two-reception probe gave the same classification failure.\\\\n\\\\nThe first call proves exactly why the engineer's claim that the endpoint is \\\\\\\"not a count\\\\nphp's machinery could have produced\\\\\\\" is false: php's own repaired ownership operations\\\\njust produced it. The value is still throwable by php/throw-value, which checks the\\\\nThrowable hierarchy and uses the repaired exact addref. Only boundary reception changes\\\\nits class/identity. An ordinary catch retaining one more reference cannot make an otherwise\\\\nunchanged local exception foreign.\\\\n\\\\n### Endpoint ruling\\\\n\\\\nSection 8.10.7 rule 1 and the owner ruling v2 R2 require already-local exceptions to retain\\\\nordinary catching and identity. Rule 3 applies to an OTHERWISE-foreign payload; it does\\\\nnot authorize reclassifying this runtime-produced state just because its private reference\\\\ncount reached the source-language integer endpoint.\\\\n\\\\nThe accepted Python implementation at prelude.cljc:1067-1079 recognizes its own exception\\\\nrepresentation/hierarchy and raises that value unchanged. It has no PHP reference-count\\\\nanalogue that would justify a signed-64-bit cutoff on PHP's now-exact ownership arithmetic.\\\\nPHP still needs representation and operability checks; that does not make every previous\\\\ncount cutoff a semantic invariant after its original arithmetic reason has been removed.\\\\n\\\\nI therefore rule the other way on the handover question: the signed-64-bit endpoints,\\\\nby themselves, are not a reason to wrap an otherwise admitted Throwable under the repaired\\\\narithmetic. The positive maximum counterexample alone is enough to reject the restored\\\\npolicy. The minimum-only exclusion likewise lacks the old decrement-overflow justification;\\\\nany separately proposed count invariant must be justified consistently, not inferred from\\\\nthe old host carrier limits.\\\\n\\\\nRequired completion:\\\\n\\\\n- Preserve exact integer/add and integer/sub and the exact-integer admission predicate.\\\\n- Make reception preserve local type/identity across ordinary acquisitions and later\\\\n  receptions, including the runtime-produced endpoint and beyond-native results.\\\\n- Extend the near-max source pin to two calls while the first result remains live, with\\\\n  original identity and original Exception class on BOTH results.\\\\n- Keep endpoint coverage, but revise its asserted disposition under this ruling instead\\\\n  of deleting the cases. Keep malformed, competing-marker, ratio, missing/non-number and\\\\n  legitimate-local controls with complete-condition/evidence/payload checks for foreign\\\\n  cases.\\\\n\\\\nThis revises the old endpoint disposition explicitly in response to the requested ruling;\\\\nit is not permission to silently invert or remove unrelated pins.\\\\n\\\\n### Do not apply the documented bare revert without a limit-aware replacement\\\\n\\\\nThe engineer is correct about the separate integer-module limit hazard: integer/integer?\\\\nrecognizes carriers without checking ::integer/max-bits; integer/add and integer/sub can\\\\nreturn ::bit-limit. Installing that keyword as :rc would make the next integer operation\\\\nrefuse. Merely deleting both bounds and accepting EVERY exact integer is not a complete fix.\\\\n\\\\nThat hazard does not justify the particular strict 64-bit boundary: MAX, MAX+1 and MIN are\\\\nwell inside the profile's minimum 4096-bit arithmetic limit. Count validation/limit handling\\\\nmust distinguish genuinely unsupported state from the local results ordinary ownership\\\\nnow produces. No broad host catch is authorized.\\\\n\\\\nTwo details in the findings' supporting argument should also be corrected:\\\\n(1) raw clojure.core/* on two Long operands does not auto-promote past overflow; a BigInt\\\\nseed is needed for the proposed repeated-squaring construction; (2) an initial 63-bit\\\\nbound does not prove ALL subsequent ownership results stay 64-bit. Neither correction\\\\nnegates the real ::bit-limit hazard, and neither is the blocking reproduction above.\\\\n\\\\n## Ruling 1 — both re-check-4 count-domain counterexamples are repaired\\\\n\\\\nphp/addref (:1856-1857) and php/delref (:1869) now use the integer module. Its checked big\\\\npath promotes operands to the host big carrier, computes exactly, and demotes to the\\\\ncanonical carrier; its fast path falls back when the safe native result range is exceeded.\\\\nThe ordinary lower-return acquisition still calls php/addref (lower.cljc:2945), so the\\\\nrepair applies after reception as required, including copying/rethrow operations using it.\\\\n\\\\nThe source receiver above now completes its FIRST near-max call with original identity;\\\\nthe previous native-long overflow is closed. The 2^53 decimal also completes with original\\\\nidentity through both calls on the JVM.\\\\n\\\\nFor JS, dao.host.integer/exact-integer? admits BigInt, promote keeps both arithmetic\\\\noperands in BigInt form, and demote produces the canonical Number/BigInt result. Thus the\\\\ncount is no longer sent to raw BigInt-plus-Number arithmetic. integer/sub demotes zero to\\\\nthe native zero, preserving the existing (= n 0) destruction test. This is verified by\\\\nreading the actual host/module implementations; no complete Node/Dart runtime green is\\\\nclaimed by this review. The decimal in the JVM pin is a Long and alone would not prove JS.\\\\n\\\\nAt :2936 integer/integer? now precedes BOTH integer/compare calls. Its implementation\\\\ndelegates directly to host/exact-integer?, so a computed JVM Ratio, float64 content, a\\\\nDouble, nil, or a non-number never reaches those comparisons. The old representation\\\\nclassifier php/int? and the broad data/number? check are gone from count admission.\\\\n\\\\nFirsthand direct reception controls, including full evidence and payload identity:\\\\n\\\\n| Count expression | ForeignException | Original local identity | Whole condition retained | Original payload retained |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| (/ 1 2) | true | false | true | true |\\\\n| (data/float64 1) | true | false | true | true |\\\\n| (data/float-value 1) | true | false | true | true |\\\\n| (/ 6 3) | false | true | n/a | n/a |\\\\n| parse MAX | true | false | true | true |\\\\n| parse MIN | true | false | true | true |\\\\n\\\\nThe last two rows record CURRENT behavior, which the endpoint ruling above rejects;\\\\nthe retention machinery itself works on those rejected paths. The computed-ratio\\\\nresidual is CLOSED, not merely hidden behind another refusal.\\\\n\\\\n## Ruling 3 — accept leaving finalize!'s raw unpin in this round\\\\n\\\\nAt prelude.cljc:4382 finalize! overwrites :rc with the literal native 1 BEFORE invoking\\\\nthe destructor. The raw subtraction at :4386 therefore does not consume the large count\\\\nadmitted from a boundary. Under ordinary PHP ownership, only acquisitions/releases made\\\\nduring this destructor contribute to its unpin value.\\\\n\\\\nI accept that scoped argument for the requested residual: the near-max and JS-BigInt\\\\nreceived-carrier counterexamples cannot flow into this subtraction. No conversion is\\\\nrequired for this review. This is not a proof against arbitrary guest cell/set! corruption\\\\nduring a destructor, nor an unrestricted totality claim for an unbounded number of\\\\nacquisitions. Neither is the counterexample established here.\\\\n\\\\n## Ruling 4 — pins, publication surface and scope\\\\n\\\\nThe requested range is one commit and exactly three changed files: the PHP prelude,\\\\nthe PHP exception test, and the r10 findings. No other frontend, VM implementation,\\\\nlowerer, shared kit, fixture, ledger, or design file changes in this range.\\\\n\\\\nNo previous assertion or payload case is removed. The two original endpoint payload\\\\nforms remain, with their foreign-retention observations; the computed-ratio case and\\\\nthe ordinary catch/return acquisition test are additive. The committed shape matrix is\\\\nnow TWELVE cases with FOUR observations each (48 expected keys per backend), rather than\\\\nthe supplied gate summary's 45-key description. I did not inspect that remote batch's\\\\nraw output and do not equate these cardinalities; the direct ratio result above is\\\\nfirsthand evidence of the repaired ratio path.\\\\n\\\\nAdding integer/integer? to host-names declares an already-existing integer module export,\\\\nnot a new PHP publication door. Removing data/number? removes an unused declaration.\\\\nThe PHP exports, aliases, reserved provenance doors, receiver/mint privacy and mint call\\\\ngraph are unchanged. There is no broad host catch or arbitrary-condition mint helper.\\\\n\\\\naddref/delref are outside the original receiver's textual region, but changing ownership\\\\noperations is expressly required by re-check 4's whole-consumption-path residual and\\\\nthis dispatch. No unrelated ownership algorithm changed. The literal surface-registry\\\\nfile 1794343000000 remains absent here, as disclosed in the original review; scope is\\\\nchecked against the available binding briefs and the actual diff, not a nonexistent\\\\nregistry artifact.\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence and remaining gate disposition\\\\n\\\\nFirsthand this round:\\\\n\\\\n- Full requested source/test diff, re-check 4, r10 findings, governing architecture,\\\\n  §8.10.7, owner ruling v2, accepted Python reception and relevant integer/ownership/\\\\n  lowering implementations inspected.\\\\n- Bounded current-source JVM diagnostics completed with exit 0: direct count controls\\\\n  and the parsed/lowered receiver called twice with three count controls.\\\\n- Static surface/adoption/reachability pins: 3 tests, 14 passes, 0 failures, 0 errors.\\\\n- git diff --check 265c4a45..59329a05 passed; tracked working-tree status remained clean.\\\\n\\\\nThe source probe borrowed only existing generated parser class directories from the\\\\nphpexc checkout, plus cached dependency jars; current worktree source/test roots led\\\\nthe classpath. PHP manifest.edn and PhpParser.g4 match that checkout byte-for-byte.\\\\nNo builds, generated files, dependency resolution or cache writes were performed.\\\\nA preliminary source-probe invocation had a diagnostic-script read error; the\\\\nreported source table is from the corrected, successful bounded invocation, not that\\\\nattempt. The parserless identity failure was independently observed as well.\\\\n\\\\nThe orchestrator's tip gate evidence in the dispatch is accepted as supplied evidence:\\\\nfocused count/nil/shape/directed/adoption/reachability batches, listed namespaces, kondo\\\\nand cljstyle. Those successes cover the existing assertions; a one-reception count pin\\\\ndoes not exercise the newly demonstrated second reception. I did not rerun the linked\\\\nbatches, contract kit, Node/Dart boundary harness, full directed matrix or foundation gate.\\\\n\\\\nThe three disclosed :php/local-reception :payload-identical lane-blocked failures retain\\\\ntheir previous disposition; no changed shared fixture/expectation contributes to the\\\\nnew failure. Existing nil-pin class/provenance coverage notes, suspension/context lifetime\\\\nand steward-owned remote/foundation obligations remain as previously recorded.\\\\n\\\\n**RESIDUALS — NOT MERGE-READY.** The arithmetic and computed-ratio repairs stand.\\\\nComplete stable local identity at subsequent reception before this PHP step-7 slice closes.\\\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-13 00:4x GMT (approximate — no shell in this session, so no `date -u`;\\\\nthe dispatch was stamped 2026-11-12 23:58:52 GMT and this round's edits followed immediately)\\\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-13 07:4x +0700 (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh)\\\\nCoding-Agent: qwen (qwen3.8-max)\\\\nSession-ID: 335-session-resume (resumed r10 session a42cc0de-3aef-43de-94de-3d9e4102bc5e)\\\\nRole: Engineer (implementation, yang.php) — r11, sol's endpoint ruling\\\\nRound: PHP r11 — the refcount endpoints are admitted local state (sol's step-7 re-check round 5,\\\\n1794527358000, read in full in this worktree) — branch exception-php-r1r2r3\\\\nReviewed-Base: 4a246994 (docs) on 59329a05 (r10) — the tree was CLEAN when this round started\\\\nStatus: SOURCE COMPLETE, **NOT GATED, NOT COMMITTED** — this session still has NO shell of any\\\\nkind, so the required two-reception before/after transcripts, the focused suites, kondo,\\\\ncljstyle and the `git commit` could not be produced. Read §1 before anything else.\\\\n\\\\n# Findings — r11: the endpoints are local, and the limit hazard is closed by probe, not by bound\\\\n\\\\n## 1. The blocking limitation, again, stated plainly\\\\n\\\\n`tool_search` for `run_shell_command` / `monitor` / `bash` / `execute_command` returned nothing\\\\nthis round either (checked twice, by name and by keyword). A subagent probe in the r10 round\\\\nreported the same absence verbatim. `computer_use` is not used as a backdoor terminal (the user\\\\ndeclined that install; project memory records not to re-prompt it), and `ask_user_question` is\\\\nunavailable in this mode.\\\\n\\\\n| Required by the r11 dispatch | Status |\\\\n| --- | --- |\\\\n| two-reception scenario FAILS before the edit, passes after — both runs captured verbatim | **NOT DONE** — impossible. §4 gives sol's own firsthand *before* table (quoted, attributed) and a source-traced *after* prediction, labelled as prediction |\\\\n| remove both endpoint exclusions from `php/obj-val?` | **DONE** |\\\\n| preserve `integer/add`/`integer/sub` and `integer/integer?` | **DONE** — untouched |\\\\n| limit-aware replacement, not a bare revert | **DONE** — §3 |\\\\n| reception preserves local type/identity across ordinary acquisitions and later receptions | **DONE** in source; **UNVERIFIED** at runtime |\\\\n| endpoint coverage revised to LOCAL, not deleted | **DONE** — §5 |\\\\n| two receptions with the first result kept live | **DONE** — `php-count-acquisition-test` |\\\\n| `php/finalize!` untouched (Ruling 3) | **DONE** — not edited |\\\\n| `host-names` declarations unchanged | **DONE** — not edited this round |\\\\n| full php-exception-test focused set green | **NOT RUN** |\\\\n| prelude pins green | **NOT RUN** |\\\\n| kondo 0/0, cljstyle clean | **NOT RUN**; formatting matched by hand to adjacent proven bytes |\\\\n| commit `fix(yang.php): the refcount endpoints are admitted local state (r11, sol's endpoint ruling)`, no push | **NOT DONE** — `git` unavailable |\\\\n\\\\nThe tree is left dirty (two modified files) exactly as r10 was; that round was gated and\\\\ncommitted by a seat with a shell (59329a05), so the same hand-off applies here. §6 lists the\\\\ncommands.\\\\n\\\\n## 2. The ruling, accepted\\\\n\\\\nsol ruled against the disposition I argued for in r10 §7 question 1, and the reproduction is\\\\ndecisive: a near-max handle is admitted and returned as the original `Exception`, its count is\\\\nleft AT the native maximum *by php's own repaired arithmetic*, and the next ordinary reception\\\\nthen excludes that endpoint and hands back a `ForeignException`. An ordinary catch retaining one\\\\nmore reference cannot make an unchanged local exception foreign. My r10 framing — \\\\\\\"not a count\\\\nphp's machinery could have produced\\\\\\\" — is falsified by the first call itself, which is php's\\\\nmachinery producing it. Accepted without reservation; the bounds are gone.\\\\n\\\\nI also accept both corrections to r10's supporting argument:\\\\n\\\\n1. **Raw `clojure.core/*` on two Long operands does not auto-promote** — it throws\\\\n   `ArithmeticException: long overflow`; only `*'` promotes. My \\\\\\\"square the long maximum a dozen\\\\n   times\\\\\\\" construction was wrong as written. (The r11 construction in §5 avoids it: the seed is\\\\n   `integer/shift-left`, which the module demotes to a `clojure.lang.BigInt`, and raw `+` on two\\\\n   BigInts goes through `Numbers$BigIntOps.add` and does not throw.)\\\\n2. **An initial 63-bit bound does not prove all later ownership results stay 64-bit** — counts\\\\n   grow by one per acquisition, so any admission-time bound is a snapshot, not an invariant.\\\\n   Correct, and it is the reason the r11 replacement proves *headroom* rather than a bound (§3).\\\\n\\\\nNeither correction negated the `::bit-limit` hazard, which sol confirms is real; §3 is the\\\\nlimit-aware replacement the dispatch and re-check5 both require.\\\\n\\\\n## 3. The limit-aware replacement: probe the arithmetic, don't bound the carrier\\\\n\\\\n`php/obj-val?`'s count admission is now three total calls, no comparison against any endpoint:\\\\n\\\\n```clojure\\\\n(if (integer/integer? rc)\\\\n  (if (integer/integer? (integer/add rc 1))\\\\n    (integer/integer? (integer/add rc rc))\\\\n    false)\\\\n  false)\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n- `integer/integer?` — the exact-integer domain predicate (r10, unchanged; residual 2's repair).\\\\n- `(integer/add rc 1)` — **addref's own operation**, so admission establishes r7's\\\\n  \\\\\\\"INCREMENTABLE\\\\\\\" property against the composition's actual arithmetic domain instead of against\\\\n  the native carrier. `int-add` runs `out`, and `out` answers the `::bit-limit` keyword once the\\\\n  result's magnitude-bit-length exceeds `::integer/max-bits`; a keyword fails `integer/integer?`,\\\\n  so such a count is FOREIGN and the keyword is never installed as `:rc` where the next ownership\\\\n  operation would refuse as a host failure.\\\\n- `(integer/add rc rc)` — **headroom**. This is the direct answer to correction 2: an\\\\n  admission-time increment is a snapshot, because counts move by one per acquisition. A\\\\n  successful doubling proves `bits(rc) + 1 <= max-bits`, i.e. `|rc| < 2^(max-bits - 1)`, so\\\\n  reaching the limit afterwards takes at least `2^(max-bits - 1)` acquisitions — 2^99999 in these\\\\n  lanes, 2^4095 at the profile floor. `integer/sub rc 1` is covered by the same headroom, so r7's\\\\n  decrementability holds without a second probe. (Probe 1 is mathematically implied by probe 2 and\\\\n  is kept because it *is* the operation being licensed; it costs one fast-path call.)\\\\n\\\\nWhy this satisfies \\\\\\\"distinguish genuinely unsupported state from the local results ordinary\\\\nownership now produces\\\\\\\", and is not the bare revert sol forbade:\\\\n\\\\n| count | bits | disposition | why |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| 1, small | ≤ 63 | LOCAL | ordinary |\\\\n| MAX-1, MAX, MIN, 2^53, 2^63, 2^64 | ≤ 65 | **LOCAL** | sol's ruling: the repaired arithmetic produces these itself; 65 ≪ 4096 ≤ max-bits, both probes answer integers |\\\\n| any beyond-native big carrier under the limit | < max-bits | LOCAL | same |\\\\n| `(/ 1 2)`, `(data/float64 1)`, `\\\\\\\"bad\\\\\\\"`, absent | — | FOREIGN | `integer/integer?` refuses (residual 2, unchanged) |\\\\n| 2^100000 in a 100000-bit lane | 100001 | FOREIGN | probe 1 answers `::bit-limit` — genuinely unsupported state |\\\\n| 2^99999 in a 100000-bit lane | 100000 | FOREIGN | probe 2 answers `::bit-limit` — no headroom |\\\\n\\\\nNo hardcoded carrier bound, no broad host catch, nothing caught after the fact, no new export or\\\\nmint surface. The thresholds come from the composition's own `::integer/max-bits`, so a lane that\\\\ninstalls different limits gets a correspondingly different admitted domain with no source change.\\\\n`host-names` is untouched: `integer/integer?` and `integer/add` were both already declared.\\\\n\\\\nThe two `php.rt/int-max` / `php.rt/int-min` reads are gone from `php/obj-val?`; both cells remain\\\\ndefined and used by `php/in-range?` (:889-890), `php/wrap64` (:909), `php/succ-key` (:1685) and\\\\n`php/to-int`'s float limit (:3137), so nothing became dead.\\\\n\\\\n## 4. The two-reception scenario: before (sol, firsthand) and after (traced, NOT observed)\\\\n\\\\n**Before — quoted from re-check5, sol's own run at tip 59329a05** (parsed/lowered source, current\\\\nworktree, production host registry, JVM AST walker, exit 0):\\\\n\\\\n| Initial count | First result identical | Count after first return | Second result identical | Second class |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| 1 | true | 2 | true | Exception |\\\\n| 9223372036854775806 | true | 9223372036854775807 | **false** | **ForeignException** |\\\\n| 9007199254740992 | true | 9007199254740993 | true | Exception |\\\\n\\\\n**After — traced from source in this tree, NOT executed. Treat every cell as a prediction.**\\\\n\\\\n| Initial count | First result | Count after first return | Second result | Second class | Reasoning |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| 9223372036854775806 | identical, `Exception` | 9223372036854775807 | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | second admission: `integer/integer? MAX` true; `integer/add MAX 1` = 2^63, bits 64 ≤ 100000 → integer; `integer/add MAX MAX` = 2^64, bits 65 → integer → LOCAL, raised unchanged, caught, `return $e` acquires again |\\\\n| 9223372036854775807 | identical, `Exception` | 2^63 (BigInt) | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | bits 64/65 at both probes |\\\\n| -9223372036854775808 | identical, `Exception` | past MIN in the big carrier | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | `integer/add MIN MIN` = -2^64, bits 65 → integer |\\\\n| 9007199254740992 | identical, `Exception` | 9007199254740993 | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | JVM Long; on JS a BigInt carrier that `promote` keeps BigInt, so no mixed `+` |\\\\n\\\\nThe mechanism that flipped the class at 59329a05 was the `(= (integer/compare rc php.rt/int-max)\\\\n-1)` clause, which is deleted here; nothing else on that path changed. **This is a source trace,\\\\nnot evidence.** The next seat must run it both ways (§6 step 1) and paste both transcripts.\\\\n\\\\n## 5. Hunk record — adopted / changed / rejected\\\\n\\\\nBase for this round: the clean r10 tree at 4a246994. Four edits, all mine; nothing from a handover.\\\\n\\\\n| # | File / site | Change | Ruling vs the dispatch |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| R1 | `prelude.cljc` `php/obj-val?` :2923-2960 | both `integer/compare` endpoint clauses **removed**; replaced by the two limit-aware probes of §3 | **as required**, plus the limit-aware replacement re-check5 demanded instead of a bare revert |\\\\n| R2 | `prelude.cljc` `php/obj-val?` docstring (count paragraph + \\\\\\\"Everything else\\\\\\\") | rewritten: operable-count reasoning, probe-not-bound, rule 1 vs rule 3, and the corrected \\\\\\\"wider than this composition's integer limits\\\\\\\" instead of \\\\\\\"at/beyond an int endpoint\\\\\\\" | **as required** (the r10 text argued for the bounds and had to go) |\\\\n| R3 | `prelude.cljc` `php/addref` docstring | r10's \\\\\\\"admission bounds it strictly inside ±2^63\\\\\\\" framing replaced: reception can put a count AT the maximum and the next acquisition past it into the big carrier; admission cuts nothing off at a carrier boundary, it probes operability | comment only; the `integer/add`/`integer/sub` bodies are **untouched**, as required |\\\\n| R4 | `php_exception_test.clj` | see below | — |\\\\n\\\\nTest file, hunk by hunk:\\\\n\\\\n| # | Hunk | Ruling |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| T1 | `max-long-count` / `min-long-count` **removed** from `php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test` | **as required** — their asserted disposition was foreign, which the ruling reverses. The payload forms are *not* deleted: both decimals reappear verbatim in `php-count-acquisition-test` (T4), so endpoint coverage is revised, not dropped |\\\\n| T2 | `float-count` shape **added** (`:rc (data/float64 1)`) | sol's own re-check5 control, firsthand-verified foreign at the reviewed tip. Host-uniform by reading: `data/float64` is `cbor/float64` → a JVM/Dart `double`, the JS `Float64` carrier (`dao.jing.cbor/float64?`), none of which `dao.host.integer/exact-integer?` admits. His *other* control, `(data/float-value 1)`, is deliberately **rejected** as a pin: on JS it yields a bare integral `Number`, which `exact-integer?` does admit, so its disposition is genuinely host-divergent and a JVM-only assertion would freeze the wrong half. Recorded in the shape's comment |\\\\n| T3 | shape-set comment block | rewritten: eleven shapes (was twelve), the count shapes now pin *domain admission* (non-number / float / ratio), and the endpoints' absence is explained with a pointer to where they are asserted instead. The stale \\\\\\\"All eight shapes\\\\\\\" phrasing stays corrected |\\\\n| T4 | `php-count-acquisition-test` **rewritten**: 2 shapes → 4 (`near-max`, `max`, `min`, `js-number-boundary`), 2 observations → 6 per shape (24 keys) | **as required.** Per shape: first reception, its value bound and kept live, its class checked, then a SECOND reception of the same callable and condition, its value and class checked. Asserts `route = :return`, `get_class == \\\\\\\"Exception\\\\\\\"` (via the contract's proven `is_foreign` idiom: a PHP-side comparison, only a boolean crosses) and `(= payload value)` on BOTH results |\\\\n| T5 | `count-source` | `is_local_exception($e) { return get_class($e) === 'Exception'; }` added beside `catch_return`. `get_class` is `php/bi-get-class`, which needs only `php/kind == :obj` and reads `:class` — it works on the minimal authentic-cell handles these shapes mint. Docstring rewritten to the r11 reasoning |\\\\n| T6 | `php-count-limit-test` **new** deftest | pins the limit-aware half: `:rc` = `(+ (integer/shift-left 1 99999) (integer/shift-left 1 99999))` = 2^100000, one bit wider than the 100000-bit `::integer/max-bits` these lanes install (`yang.typescript.antlr.linked-harness/integer-limits`, which is what `yang.call.conformance/registry` admits). Asserts the four foreign observations — retained, `is_foreign`, whole condition incl. `{:complete [:evidence]}`, payload identity — through the proven `run-provenance-observation` harness. **Kept in its own deftest on purpose**: its width is lane-specific, and if a future lane changes the limits this fails alone instead of taking the eleven-shape matrix with it |\\\\n| T7 | `php/finalize!` | **not touched** (Ruling 3) |\\\\n| T8 | `host-names` | **not touched**, as the dispatch specifies. Note the r10 cleanup (removing the then-unused `data/number?`) stands committed and is still correct: `data/number?` has no caller in `src/cljc/yang/php` |\\\\n| T9 | everything else (`php-kit-parity-test`, `php-kit-batch-parity-test`, the directed legs, forgery/provenance/nil tests, `egress-source`, `injected`, `boundary-call`, the shared kit, fixtures) | **untouched**, so the disclosed lane-blocked assertions cannot have moved |\\\\n\\\\nInterpretation I had to choose alone (`ask_user_question` unavailable): the dispatch says the\\\\nendpoint shapes must assert \\\\\\\"class Exception, payload-identical, **condition-identical where\\\\nretained-local**\\\\\\\". A local delivery retains no condition — the payload *is* the delivery — so I\\\\nread that clause as applying to the retained-foreign cases, which keep the whole-condition and\\\\nevidence assertions (T6, and the eleven-shape matrix), and pinned class + payload identity on\\\\nboth receptions for the local ones (T4). If sol meant an additional condition-level observation\\\\non the local path, say which and it is a small addition.\\\\n\\\\n## 6. What the next seat must run, in order\\\\n\\\\nNothing below was executed. Foreground, explicit timeouts, nothing backgrounded, focused\\\\nnamespaces only; `yang.call.exception-contract-test` NEVER locally.\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\ncd /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-phpexc\\\\ngit rev-parse HEAD && git status --porcelain   # expect HEAD 4a246994, M on exactly the two files\\\\n\\\\n# 1. THE REQUIRED BEFORE/AFTER, both verbatim in the findings.\\\\n#    BEFORE: stash these edits (git stash) so the tree is 4a246994 again, run the two-reception\\\\n#    probe, restore (git stash pop). Expect sol's table: near-max second result NOT identical,\\\\n#    class ForeignException.\\\\n#    AFTER: run the same probe on this tree. Expect all four counts identical with class\\\\n#    Exception on BOTH receptions.\\\\n#    A parserless direct-reception probe is enough if the parsed receiver is slow to stand up —\\\\n#    sol used one as a cross-check — but the dispatch asks for the