ns captured verbatim | **NOT DONE** — impossible. §4 gives sol's own firsthand *before* table (quoted, attributed) and a source-traced *after* prediction, labelled as prediction |\\\\n| remove both endpoint exclusions from `php/obj-val?` | **DONE** |\\\\n| preserve `integer/add`/`integer/sub` and `integer/integer?` | **DONE** — untouched |\\\\n| limit-aware replacement, not a bare revert | **DONE** — §3 |\\\\n| reception preserves local type/identity across ordinary acquisitions and later receptions | **DONE** in source; **UNVERIFIED** at runtime |\\\\n| endpoint coverage revised to LOCAL, not deleted | **DONE** — §5 |\\\\n| two receptions with the first result kept live | **DONE** — `php-count-acquisition-test` |\\\\n| `php/finalize!` untouched (Ruling 3) | **DONE** — not edited |\\\\n| `host-names` declarations unchanged | **DONE** — not edited this round |\\\\n| full php-exception-test focused set green | **NOT RUN** |\\\\n| prelude pins green | **NOT RUN** |\\\\n| kondo 0/0, cljstyle clean | **NOT RUN**; formatting matched by hand to adjacent proven bytes |\\\\n| commit `fix(yang.php): the refcount endpoints are admitted local state (r11, sol's endpoint ruling)`, no push | **NOT DONE** — `git` unavailable |\\\\n\\\\nThe tree is left dirty (two modified files) exactly as r10 was; that round was gated and\\\\ncommitted by a seat with a shell (59329a05), so the same hand-off applies here. §6 lists the\\\\ncommands.\\\\n\\\\n## 2. The ruling, accepted\\\\n\\\\nsol ruled against the disposition I argued for in r10 §7 question 1, and the reproduction is\\\\ndecisive: a near-max handle is admitted and returned as the original `Exception`, its count is\\\\nleft AT the native maximum *by php's own repaired arithmetic*, and the next ordinary reception\\\\nthen excludes that endpoint and hands back a `ForeignException`. An ordinary catch retaining one\\\\nmore reference cannot make an unchanged local exception foreign. My r10 framing — \\\\\\\"not a count\\\\nphp's machinery could have produced\\\\\\\" — is falsified by the first call itself, which is php's\\\\nmachinery producing it. Accepted without reservation; the bounds are gone.\\\\n\\\\nI also accept both corrections to r10's supporting argument:\\\\n\\\\n1. **Raw `clojure.core/*` on two Long operands does not auto-promote** — it throws\\\\n   `ArithmeticException: long overflow`; only `*'` promotes. My \\\\\\\"square the long maximum a dozen\\\\n   times\\\\\\\" construction was wrong as written. (The r11 construction in §5 avoids it: the seed is\\\\n   `integer/shift-left`, which the module demotes to a `clojure.lang.BigInt`, and raw `+` on two\\\\n   BigInts goes through `Numbers$BigIntOps.add` and does not throw.)\\\\n2. **An initial 63-bit bound does not prove all later ownership results stay 64-bit** — counts\\\\n   grow by one per acquisition, so any admission-time bound is a snapshot, not an invariant.\\\\n   Correct, and it is the reason the r11 replacement proves *headroom* rather than a bound (§3).\\\\n\\\\nNeither correction negated the `::bit-limit` hazard, which sol confirms is real; §3 is the\\\\nlimit-aware replacement the dispatch and re-check5 both require.\\\\n\\\\n## 3. The limit-aware replacement: probe the arithmetic, don't bound the carrier\\\\n\\\\n`php/obj-val?`'s count admission is now three total calls, no comparison against any endpoint:\\\\n\\\\n```clojure\\\\n(if (integer/integer? rc)\\\\n  (if (integer/integer? (integer/add rc 1))\\\\n    (integer/integer? (integer/add rc rc))\\\\n    false)\\\\n  false)\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n- `integer/integer?` — the exact-integer domain predicate (r10, unchanged; residual 2's repair).\\\\n- `(integer/add rc 1)` — **addref's own operation**, so admission establishes r7's\\\\n  \\\\\\\"INCREMENTABLE\\\\\\\" property against the composition's actual arithmetic domain instead of against\\\\n  the native carrier. `int-add` runs `out`, and `out` answers the `::bit-limit` keyword once the\\\\n  result's magnitude-bit-length exceeds `::integer/max-bits`; a keyword fails `integer/integer?`,\\\\n  so such a count is FOREIGN and the keyword is never installed as `:rc` where the next ownership\\\\n  operation would refuse as a host failure.\\\\n- `(integer/add rc rc)` — **headroom**. This is the direct answer to correction 2: an\\\\n  admission-time increment is a snapshot, because counts move by one per acquisition. A\\\\n  successful doubling proves `bits(rc) + 1 <= max-bits`, i.e. `|rc| < 2^(max-bits - 1)`, so\\\\n  reaching the limit afterwards takes at least `2^(max-bits - 1)` acquisitions — 2^99999 in these\\\\n  lanes, 2^4095 at the profile floor. `integer/sub rc 1` is covered by the same headroom, so r7's\\\\n  decrementability holds without a second probe. (Probe 1 is mathematically implied by probe 2 and\\\\n  is kept because it *is* the operation being licensed; it costs one fast-path call.)\\\\n\\\\nWhy this satisfies \\\\\\\"distinguish genuinely unsupported state from the local results ordinary\\\\nownership now produces\\\\\\\", and is not the bare revert sol forbade:\\\\n\\\\n| count | bits | disposition | why |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| 1, small | ≤ 63 | LOCAL | ordinary |\\\\n| MAX-1, MAX, MIN, 2^53, 2^63, 2^64 | ≤ 65 | **LOCAL** | sol's ruling: the repaired arithmetic produces these itself; 65 ≪ 4096 ≤ max-bits, both probes answer integers |\\\\n| any beyond-native big carrier under the limit | < max-bits | LOCAL | same |\\\\n| `(/ 1 2)`, `(data/float64 1)`, `\\\\\\\"bad\\\\\\\"`, absent | — | FOREIGN | `integer/integer?` refuses (residual 2, unchanged) |\\\\n| 2^100000 in a 100000-bit lane | 100001 | FOREIGN | probe 1 answers `::bit-limit` — genuinely unsupported state |\\\\n| 2^99999 in a 100000-bit lane | 100000 | FOREIGN | probe 2 answers `::bit-limit` — no headroom |\\\\n\\\\nNo hardcoded carrier bound, no broad host catch, nothing caught after the fact, no new export or\\\\nmint surface. The thresholds come from the composition's own `::integer/max-bits`, so a lane that\\\\ninstalls different limits gets a correspondingly different admitted domain with no source change.\\\\n`host-names` is untouched: `integer/integer?` and `integer/add` were both already declared.\\\\n\\\\nThe two `php.rt/int-max` / `php.rt/int-min` reads are gone from `php/obj-val?`; both cells remain\\\\ndefined and used by `php/in-range?` (:889-890), `php/wrap64` (:909), `php/succ-key` (:1685) and\\\\n`php/to-int`'s float limit (:3137), so nothing became dead.\\\\n\\\\n## 4. The two-reception scenario: before (sol, firsthand) and after (traced, NOT observed)\\\\n\\\\n**Before — quoted from re-check5, sol's own run at tip 59329a05** (parsed/lowered source, current\\\\nworktree, production host registry, JVM AST walker, exit 0):\\\\n\\\\n| Initial count | First result identical | Count after first return | Second result identical | Second class |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| 1 | true | 2 | true | Exception |\\\\n| 9223372036854775806 | true | 9223372036854775807 | **false** | **ForeignException** |\\\\n| 9007199254740992 | true | 9007199254740993 | true | Exception |\\\\n\\\\n**After — traced from source in this tree, NOT executed. Treat every cell as a prediction.**\\\\n\\\\n| Initial count | First result | Count after first return | Second result | Second class | Reasoning |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| 9223372036854775806 | identical, `Exception` | 9223372036854775807 | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | second admission: `integer/integer? MAX` true; `integer/add MAX 1` = 2^63, bits 64 ≤ 100000 → integer; `integer/add MAX MAX` = 2^64, bits 65 → integer → LOCAL, raised unchanged, caught, `return $e` acquires again |\\\\n| 9223372036854775807 | identical, `Exception` | 2^63 (BigInt) | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | bits 64/65 at both probes |\\\\n| -9223372036854775808 | identical, `Exception` | past MIN in the big carrier | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | `integer/add MIN MIN` = -2^64, bits 65 → integer |\\\\n| 9007199254740992 | identical, `Exception` | 9007199254740993 | predicted identical | predicted `Exception` | JVM Long; on JS a BigInt carrier that `promote` keeps BigInt, so no mixed `+` |\\\\n\\\\nThe mechanism that flipped the class at 59329a05 was the `(= (integer/compare rc php.rt/int-max)\\\\n-1)` clause, which is deleted here; nothing else on that path changed. **This is a source trace,\\\\nnot evidence.** The next seat must run it both ways (§6 step 1) and paste both transcripts.\\\\n\\\\n## 5. Hunk record — adopted / changed / rejected\\\\n\\\\nBase for this round: the clean r10 tree at 4a246994. Four edits, all mine; nothing from a handover.\\\\n\\\\n| # | File / site | Change | Ruling vs the dispatch |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| R1 | `prelude.cljc` `php/obj-val?` :2923-2960 | both `integer/compare` endpoint clauses **removed**; replaced by the two limit-aware probes of §3 | **as required**, plus the limit-aware replacement re-check5 demanded instead of a bare revert |\\\\n| R2 | `prelude.cljc` `php/obj-val?` docstring (count paragraph + \\\\\\\"Everything else\\\\\\\") | rewritten: operable-count reasoning, probe-not-bound, rule 1 vs rule 3, and the corrected \\\\\\\"wider than this composition's integer limits\\\\\\\" instead of \\\\\\\"at/beyond an int endpoint\\\\\\\" | **as required** (the r10 text argued for the bounds and had to go) |\\\\n| R3 | `prelude.cljc` `php/addref` docstring | r10's \\\\\\\"admission bounds it strictly inside ±2^63\\\\\\\" framing replaced: reception can put a count AT the maximum and the next acquisition past it into the big carrier; admission cuts nothing off at a carrier boundary, it probes operability | comment only; the `integer/add`/`integer/sub` bodies are **untouched**, as required |\\\\n| R4 | `php_exception_test.clj` | see below | — |\\\\n\\\\nTest file, hunk by hunk:\\\\n\\\\n| # | Hunk | Ruling |\\\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\\\n| T1 | `max-long-count` / `min-long-count` **removed** from `php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test` | **as required** — their asserted disposition was foreign, which the ruling reverses. The payload forms are *not* deleted: both decimals reappear verbatim in `php-count-acquisition-test` (T4), so endpoint coverage is revised, not dropped |\\\\n| T2 | `float-count` shape **added** (`:rc (data/float64 1)`) | sol's own re-check5 control, firsthand-verified foreign at the reviewed tip. Host-uniform by reading: `data/float64` is `cbor/float64` → a JVM/Dart `double`, the JS `Float64` carrier (`dao.jing.cbor/float64?`), none of which `dao.host.integer/exact-integer?` admits. His *other* control, `(data/float-value 1)`, is deliberately **rejected** as a pin: on JS it yields a bare integral `Number`, which `exact-integer?` does admit, so its disposition is genuinely host-divergent and a JVM-only assertion would freeze the wrong half. Recorded in the shape's comment |\\\\n| T3 | shape-set comment block | rewritten: eleven shapes (was twelve), the count shapes now pin *domain admission* (non-number / float / ratio), and the endpoints' absence is explained with a pointer to where they are asserted instead. The stale \\\\\\\"All eight shapes\\\\\\\" phrasing stays corrected |\\\\n| T4 | `php-count-acquisition-test` **rewritten**: 2 shapes → 4 (`near-max`, `max`, `min`, `js-number-boundary`), 2 observations → 6 per shape (24 keys) | **as required.** Per shape: first reception, its value bound and kept live, its class checked, then a SECOND reception of the same callable and condition, its value and class checked. Asserts `route = :return`, `get_class == \\\\\\\"Exception\\\\\\\"` (via the contract's proven `is_foreign` idiom: a PHP-side comparison, only a boolean crosses) and `(= payload value)` on BOTH results |\\\\n| T5 | `count-source` | `is_local_exception($e) { return get_class($e) === 'Exception'; }` added beside `catch_return`. `get_class` is `php/bi-get-class`, which needs only `php/kind == :obj` and reads `:class` — it works on the minimal authentic-cell handles these shapes mint. Docstring rewritten to the r11 reasoning |\\\\n| T6 | `php-count-limit-test` **new** deftest | pins the limit-aware half: `:rc` = `(+ (integer/shift-left 1 99999) (integer/shift-left 1 99999))` = 2^100000, one bit wider than the 100000-bit `::integer/max-bits` these lanes install (`yang.typescript.antlr.linked-harness/integer-limits`, which is what `yang.call.conformance/registry` admits). Asserts the four foreign observations — retained, `is_foreign`, whole condition incl. `{:complete [:evidence]}`, payload identity — through the proven `run-provenance-observation` harness. **Kept in its own deftest on purpose**: its width is lane-specific, and if a future lane changes the limits this fails alone instead of taking the eleven-shape matrix with it |\\\\n| T7 | `php/finalize!` | **not touched** (Ruling 3) |\\\\n| T8 | `host-names` | **not touched**, as the dispatch specifies. Note the r10 cleanup (removing the then-unused `data/number?`) stands committed and is still correct: `data/number?` has no caller in `src/cljc/yang/php` |\\\\n| T9 | everything else (`php-kit-parity-test`, `php-kit-batch-parity-test`, the directed legs, forgery/provenance/nil tests, `egress-source`, `injected`, `boundary-call`, the shared kit, fixtures) | **untouched**, so the disclosed lane-blocked assertions cannot have moved |\\\\n\\\\nInterpretation I had to choose alone (`ask_user_question` unavailable): the dispatch says the\\\\nendpoint shapes must assert \\\\\\\"class Exception, payload-identical, **condition-identical where\\\\nretained-local**\\\\\\\". A local delivery retains no condition — the payload *is* the delivery — so I\\\\nread that clause as applying to the retained-foreign cases, which keep the whole-condition and\\\\nevidence assertions (T6, and the eleven-shape matrix), and pinned class + payload identity on\\\\nboth receptions for the local ones (T4). If sol meant an additional condition-level observation\\\\non the local path, say which and it is a small addition.\\\\n\\\\n## 6. What the next seat must run, in order\\\\n\\\\nNothing below was executed. Foreground, explicit timeouts, nothing backgrounded, focused\\\\nnamespaces only; `yang.call.exception-contract-test` NEVER locally.\\\\n\\\\n```sh\\\\ncd /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-phpexc\\\\ngit rev-parse HEAD && git status --porcelain   # expect HEAD 4a246994, M on exactly the two files\\\\n\\\\n# 1. THE REQUIRED BEFORE/AFTER, both verbatim in the findings.\\\\n#    BEFORE: stash these edits (git stash) so the tree is 4a246994 again, run the two-reception\\\\n#    probe, restore (git stash pop). Expect sol's table: near-max second result NOT identical,\\\\n#    class ForeignException.\\\\n#    AFTER: run the same probe on this tree. Expect all four counts identical with class\\\\n#    Exception on BOTH receptions.\\\\n#    A parserless direct-reception probe is enough if the parsed receiver is slow to stand up —\\\\n#    sol used one as a cross-check — but the dispatch asks for the source receiver, so run both\\\\n#    if the lane allows.\\\\ntimeout 900 clojure -M:test -e \\\\\\\\\\\\n '(do (require (quote [clojure.test :as t])) \\\\\\\\\\\\n      (require (quote yang.call.php-exception-test)) \\\\\\\\\\\\n      (t/run-tests (quote yang.call.php-exception-test)))'\\\\n# expected new/changed counts: php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test 11 shapes x 4 = 44 keys\\\\n# per backend; php-count-acquisition-test 4 shapes x 6 = 24 keys per backend;\\\\n# php-count-limit-test 4 keys per backend. Everything else unchanged.\\\\n# the focused namespaces r7/r10 used, at minimum:\\\\n#   yang.call.php-exception-test, yang.php.antlr.prelude-test, yang.php.antlr.class-model-test,\\\\n#   yang.php.antlr.objects-test, yang.php.antlr.generators-test,\\\\n#   yang.php.antlr.builtin-surfaces-test, yang.php.antlr.e2e-fast-test,\\\\n#   yang.php.antlr.exception-source-test, dao.host.conformance-test, yin.vm.integer-test\\\\ntimeout 300 clojure -M:clj-kondo --lint src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc \\\\\\\\\\\\n                                        test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj   # expect 0/0\\\\ntimeout 300 clojure -M:cljstyle check src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc \\\\\\\\\\\\n                                      test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj     # expect clean\\\\ngit add src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj \\\\\\\\\\\\n        collab/exception-track/1794527932000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r11.qwen3.8-max.findings.md\\\\ngit commit -m \\\\\\\"fix(yang.php): the refcount endpoints are admitted local state (r11, sol's endpoint ruling)\\\\\\\"\\\\n# DO NOT PUSH — the orchestrator gates on jupiter and pushes.\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nTwo things to watch when it runs, both unrunnable from here:\\\\n\\\\n- **`float-count` and `over-limit-count` are new and unverified.** If `float-count` surprises, the\\\\n  suspect is the observation-lane representation of `data/float64`; if `php-count-limit-test`\\\\n  surprises, first print `(integer/shift-left 1 99999)` and the lane's `::integer/max-bits` — the\\\\n  construction assumes exactly 100000 and is one bit over it.\\\\n- **`php-count-acquisition-test` now makes four boundary calls per shape** (16 total in one\\\\n  composed program, up from 4). The eleven-shape matrix already runs 33 in one program and is\\\\n  green, so this should be inside the lane's tolerance, but it is the one place where the round\\\\n  adds run time rather than moving it.\\\\n\\\\n## 7. Residuals and carry-overs\\\\n\\\\n- The `::bit-limit` hazard is now closed by probe at admission, and the headroom probe closes the\\\\n  creep case sol's correction 2 exposed. What remains, and is *not* claimed closed: arbitrary\\\\n  guest `cell/set!` corruption of a live count mid-lifetime (sol already scoped that out of\\\\n  Ruling 3), and an unlimited number of acquisitions on a count admitted with zero headroom —\\\\n  which the doub