hed | **NOT DONE** — `git` is unavailable |\\\\\\\\n| findings artifact | this file |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe dispatch said \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Produce the COMPLETE deliverable in this run; do not wait for approval.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" The\\\\\\\\ncomplete deliverable *that this session can produce* is the reviewed-and-corrected working tree\\\\\\\\nplus this document. The commit and the gates need a seat with a shell; §6 lists the exact\\\\\\\\ncommands. **Do not treat any number in this file as a gate result — there are none.**\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 2. The handover delta, hunk by hunk: adopted vs changed\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe interrupted session left two modified files. `git diff` was unavailable, so the delta was\\\\\\\\nrecovered two ways and cross-checked: (a) by reading the working copy against the committed\\\\\\\\nbehaviour documented in `1794488073000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r7.glm-5.3.findings.md` and against\\\\\\\\n`1794516140000-architect-step7-recheck4.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`; then (b) against **HEAD's actual\\\\\\\\nbytes**, which turned out to be readable after all — `/private/tmp/phpexc-review` is a detached\\\\\\\\nworktree of this repo pinned at `265c4a4590259f2ea592cb81cd6991aa7b8afde8` (its `.git` file →\\\\\\\\n`gitdir: /private/tmp/phpexc-review/.git`, whose `HEAD` holds the literal SHA), and\\\\\\\\n`/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.git/refs/heads/exception-php-r1r2r3` = the same SHA, confirming\\\\\\\\nthis worktree's HEAD. The r9 stdout log is a one-line `[claude-code:unrecognized model]` stub with\\\\\\\\nno transcript and no diff, so it contributed nothing.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe complete handover delta is **six hunks** — three per file, all of them reviewed below, none\\\\\\\\nmissed:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- `prelude.cljc`: `host-names` :103; `php/addref` + `php/delref` :1834-1852 → :1835-1871;\\\\\\\\n  `php/obj-val?` :2836-2903 → :2849-2943.\\\\\\\\n- `php_exception_test.clj`: the two r7 boundary shapes deleted from\\\\\\\\n  `php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test` (HEAD :505-523) and `ratio-count` added in their slot;\\\\\\\\n  `count-source` added; `php-count-acquisition-test` added.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAgainst HEAD, *this round's* tree therefore differs in four places in `prelude.cljc` — the three\\\\\\\\nhandover hunks (with P4-P7 rewriting two of their comments and P6 half-reverting the third) plus\\\\\\\\nmy P8 token removal at :102 — and in three in `php_exception_test.clj` (the handover's two new\\\\\\\\ndefs kept, the deleted shapes restored). A `git diff HEAD` will show more comment churn than code\\\\\\\\nchurn; that is deliberate, because the reasoning the handover wrote down was the part that was\\\\\\\\nwrong.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOne correction to an earlier assumption of mine, now settled by HEAD's bytes: **r7 had no\\\\\\\\nnear-boundary (MAX-1) shape.** r7's three added shapes were `max-long-count`, `min-long-count`\\\\\\\\nand `non-number-count`. MAX-1 is sol's re-check4 counterexample and is pinned here for the first\\\\\\\\ntime. The two endpoint shapes are restored **byte-identical to HEAD** (payload forms included);\\\\\\\\nonly their comments were updated, because r7's rationale text named the raw host arithmetic that\\\\\\\\nr10 replaces.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### prelude.cljc\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| # | Hunk (working copy at handover) | Ruling | Why |\\\\\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\\\\\n| P1 | `host-names` (:103) extended with `integer/integer?` | **ADOPTED verbatim** | it is an export of the `integer` module (`yin.vm.integer` `exports`, `['integer? [1] int-integer?]`), so `admit` and the linker's free-name check are satisfied wherever the profile's own `register-integer-module` ran. Required: without it, publishing the module refuses `integer/integer?` as an undeclared free name. `integer/add`/`sub`/`compare` were already declared (used at :909, :959, :1194, :1685, :5451 …). |\\\\\\\\n| P2 | `php/addref` body: raw `(+ rc 1)` → `(integer/add … 1)` | **ADOPTED verbatim** | this is residual 1's repair. `int-add` promotes both operands with `host/promote` and demotes the result (`yin.vm.integer` :190, `dao.host.integer` `add`/`promote`/`demote`), so the JVM long maximum no longer overflows and a JS BigInt count no longer mixes with a Number `1`. |\\\\\\\\n| P3 | `php/delref` body: raw `(- rc 1)` → `(integer/sub … 1)` | **ADOPTED verbatim** | same domain for the decrement, which is the operation the wrapper's lifetime runs. |\\\\\\\\n| P4 | `php/addref` docstring (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"total over every exact-integer carrier … no bounds check remains … min-integer-bits is far beyond the native carriers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") | **CHANGED — rewritten** | the reasoning was wrong in a way that matters (see §3): `min-integer-bits` is a *limit*, not a safety margin, and `integer/add` answers the `::bit-limit` keyword past it. Rewritten to say what actually makes the path safe (promote/demote exactness) and why a bound alone proves nothing (MAX-1 → MAX at reception → past MAX at the lowerer's return acquisition). |\\\\\\\\n| P5 | `php/delref` had no comment | **CHANGED — 3 lines added** | records the invariant the `(= n 0)` test depends on: `integer/sub` demotes any result inside the host's native range, so a count that reaches 0 is in the native carrier on every host and the zero test is carrier-uniform. (On JS a BigInt never `=` a Number, so this is load-bearing, not decorative.) |\\\\\\\\n| P6 | `php/obj-val?` count check: HEAD's `data/number?` + `php/int?` + strict `integer/compare` bounds → bare `(integer/integer? (get st :rc))` | **CHANGED — half adopted, half reverted.** Kept `integer/integer?` *as the domain predicate* (that half is residual 2's repair and is correct); **restored r7's strict bounds** inside it. Final shape is HEAD's own nesting (`(let [st …]` → class check → inner `(let [rc …]`), so the code diff against 265c4a45 is one collapsed predicate level, not a restructuring | Dropping `data/number?`+`php/int?` is right and is adopted: `php/int?` is a representation classifier (it answers `:int` for a computed ratio — the re-check4 counterexample — and on JS `php/kind`'s `data/content=` fallthrough compares a BigInt count against its own float64 image, which is exactly 2^53's value, so it could classify the *required* JS-boundary count as `:float`). Dropping the bounds is wrong: see §3 (a new host-failure escape) and §4 (it inverts two committed r7 controls, which the dispatch requires to stay green). HEAD's inline comment explaining why `data/number?` had to precede `php/int?` (php/kind's non-number fallthrough calls `data/float-value` and refuses on absent counts) is replaced by one explaining why the domain predicate has to precede `integer/compare`; with `php/int?` gone, that refusal path is gone too — `integer/integer?` is total and answers false for nil. |\\\\\\\\n| P7 | `php/obj-val?` docstring, count paragraph + \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Everything else\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" paragraph | **CHANGED — rewritten** | now states both checks and the question each answers: the domain predicate makes the count *operable* for its whole ownership life; the bounds keep it inside the composition's integer *limits* and route endpoint state the safe way (foreign, retained whole, never consumed). r9's rewrite of the docstring's *opening* paragraph (extending the sweep from \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the reception path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"every ordinary acquisition after that — a caught local value returned by php source runs the lowerer's php/addref; copies and rethrows acquire too\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") is **ADOPTED**: it is the correction re-check4 demanded, and it is accurate. |\\\\\\\\n| P8 | *(mine, not in the handover)* `data/number?` **removed** from `host-names` (:102) | **NEW — cleanup of a dead declaration this round created** | P6 dropped the only caller: `grep number? src/cljc/yang/php` now answers just the declaration and two comments, so `host-names` (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Module exports the prelude calls\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") was declaring an export nothing calls. Statically safe, and I checked every pin that reads the list rather than assuming: `bundled-prelude-is-closed-test` is one-directional (`free-names ⊆ defined-keys ∪ host-names ∪ primitives`), so shrinking the set cannot break it and `integer/integer?` **had** to be added for it (the integer module is never in `vm/primitives`); `prelude-reaches-no-dao-host-test` needs every entry's namespace in `#{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"integer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"stream\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}` — `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` and `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"integer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` both qualify; `module-spec-declares-every-free-name-test` only filters the `integer/*` entries and requires each to be in the spec's primitives, which `register-integer-module` supplies for all 26 exports; `admit-refuses-a-registry-without-the-host-names-test` calls `(prelude/admit {})`, which still refuses on the remaining names. `portable_harness/host-registry` registers the four modules wholesale and then admits, so it never enumerated the list either. If a gate I could not run contradicts this, restoring the one token is the whole revert. |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nUnchanged by me and walked as consumers (r7's sweep, re-verified against the current bytes):\\\\\\\\n`php/finalize!` (:4341-4353) still holds a raw `(- (get st :rc) 1)`. It is **safe and was left\\\\\\\\nalone**: the line immediately before it pins the cell to the literal `:rc 1`, so the only\\\\\\\\nincrements the raw subtraction can ever see are acquisitions made during the destructor, and\\\\\\\\nreaching the long maximum from 1 would take 2^63 of them. Converting it would be an untestable\\\\\\\\nedit to the destroy path for no reachable gain; disclosed here so the next re-check does not\\\\\\\\nhave to re-derive it. `php/rc-of`, `php/dup-slot`'s `(= (get st :rc) 1)` and `php/shutdown-pass`\\\\\\\\nare equality/reads, total. `php.rt/mint-retained` (:4706ff) mints `:rc 1` — engine-owned\\\\\\\\ncomplete state. `lower-return` (`lower.cljc` :2945) wraps every returned expression in\\\\\\\\n`php/addref`, which is now the exact domain: that is the second acquisition in sol's\\\\\\\\ncounterexample and it is closed by P2.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| # | Hunk | Ruling | Why |\\\\\\\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\\\\\\\n| T1 | `count-source` (new `^:private` def): `<?php function catch_return($f) { try { return $f(); } catch (Throwable $e) { return $e; } }` | **ADOPTED** (docstring rewritten) | sol's own receiver source, verbatim in behaviour: an ordinary catch/return with no numeric operation anywhere, whose `return $e` runs the lowerer's acquisition. The harness pattern is already proven in this file — `run-egress-observation` passes its own `egress-source` to `kit/compose-exception` the same way, and two green tests use it. |\\\\\\\\n| T2 | `php-count-acquisition-test` (new deftest): per-shape bindings → `catch_return` → `{:…-returned :…-identical}` | **ADOPTED structure; CHANGED shape set 4 → 2** | kept `near-max` (9223372036854775806) and `js-number-boundary` (9007199254740992) — the two counterexamples sol asked to add. **Removed `max` and `min`**: with the bounds restored they are foreign, not local, and they are pinned as foreign in T3 where the full retention observations live. Asserting them local here would have contradicted the dispatch's \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the r7 boundary controls stay green\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\". |\\\\\\\\n| T3 | `max-long-count` / `min-long-count` shapes **deleted** from `php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test` | **REVERTED — both restored, byte-identical to HEAD :505-523** (payload forms included; only their comment blocks updated, since r7's rationale named the raw host arithmetic r10 replaces) | these are r7's committed boundary controls (11 shapes, 44 keys, green at 265c4a45). Deleting them and re-asserting the opposite disposition elsewhere is a pin inversion, which every prior re-check has treated as a weakening. Restored in r7's position (after `string-class-no-rc`, before `non-number-count`). |\\\\\\\\n| T4 | `ratio-count` shape added (`:rc (/ 1 2)`) | **ADOPTED verbatim** | the residual-2 pin sol required: computed rational, admitted as FOREIGN with the complete condition, its evidence and original payload identity (the four standard observations). Comment re-stamped r9 → r10. |\\\\\\\\n| T5 | `non-number-count` shape kept but displaced after `ratio-count` | **ADOPTED — restored to its HEAD position** | r6's rule; back in r7's committed order, with `ratio-count` appended last → twelve shapes, 48 keys, still ONE composed program (the shape list drives `bindings`, `entries` and `expected`, so the expected map is computed, not hand-counted). |\\\\\\\\n| T6 | deftest comment \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"All eight shapes run in ONE composed program … catches the 8th consecutive per-shape run\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | **CHANGED** | stale since r7 (sol flagged it in re-check4: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The old 'all eight' comment is stale\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"). Now says twelve, and records what the r7 endpoint shapes pin and why r10 keeps them. |\\\\\\\\n| T7 | comment \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"addref's `(+ rc 1)` consumes it\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | **CHANGED** | the code no longer says that; now \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"addref's count acquisition consumes it\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\". |\\\\\\\\n| T8 | everything else in the file (`php-kit-parity-test`, `php-kit-batch-parity-test`, the directed legs, the forgery/provenance/nil tests, `egress-source`, `injected`, `boundary-call`) | **UNTOUCHED** | byte-identical, so the disclosed lane-blocked assertions cannot have moved. |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 3. The defect I found in the handover that neither r9 nor re-check4 identified\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe handover's admission was `(integer/integer? rc)` and nothing else, on the stated reasoning\\\\\\\\nthat \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"counts at or beyond the native carriers are inside php's own integer model …\\\\\\\\n(min-integer-bits is far beyond the native carriers) … the arithmetic itself cannot overflow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\".\\\\\\\\n`integer/add` is exact, but it is **not total**: it is *limited*.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```clojure\\\\\\\\n;; yin.vm.integer\\\\\\\\n(defn- out [limits b] (if (> (bits b) (::max-bits limits)) ::bit-limit (host/demote b)))\\\\\\\\n(defn- int-add [limits a b] (out limits (host/add (big! 'add 0 a) (big! 'add 1 b))))\\\\\\\\n(defn- big! [op arg x] (if (host/exact-integer? x) (host/promote x) (wrong-type! op arg :integer)))\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nand `::integer/max-bits` is finite for every PHP composition: the profile's floor\\\\\\\\n`yang.php.antlr.prelude/min-integer-bits` is **4096** (`register-integer-module` refuses\\\\\\\\nanything less), and the compositions these tests build install **100000** —\\\\\\\\n`test/yang/php/antlr/portable_harness.cljc:78`, `{::integer/max-bits 100000,\\\\\\\\n::integer/max-digits 4300}`. Whichever number applies, it is the point past which `integer/add`\\\\\\\\nstops answering an integer and answers the keyword `:yin.vm.integer/bit-limit` instead. Meanwhile\\\\\\\\nthe VM's arithmetic primitives are raw host references (`yin.vm/primitives`: `(pure '+ + …)`,\\\\\\\\n`(pure '- - …)`, `(pure '* * …)`), which no `::max-bits` bounds and which auto-promote on the\\\\\\\\nJVM — the same fact that let sol build a Ratio with `(/ 1 2)`. So a guest can build an\\\\\\\\narbitrarily wide exact-integer count in a handful of operations (`(* x x)` from the long maximum:\\\\\\\\n63 → 126 → 252 → … bits, i.e. past the 4096 floor in six squarings and past 100000 bits in\\\\\\\\neleven), and then:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. `integer/integer?` admits it (a `clojure.lang.BigInt` *is* an exact-integer carrier);\\\\\\\\n2. reception's `php/addref` calls `integer/add`, which answers `::bit-limit`;\\\\\\\\n3. that **keyword is `assoc`'d into the cell as `:rc`** and the payload is raised as a local\\\\\\\\n   Throwable — no refusal, nothing observable yet;\\\\\\\\n4. the next ownership operation on that count (`php/delref`'s `integer/sub`, or any\\\\\\\\n   `integer/compare`) reaches `big!` with a keyword and throws `ex-info` `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"integer primitive\\\\\\\\n   refused\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` `{:yin.vm.integer/reason :wrong-type, :op sub, :arg 0, :expected :integer}` — a\\\\\\\\n   **host failure escaping the boundary**, which is precisely the class this whole round exists\\\\\\\\n   to eliminate (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a caller checks for them before the call, so reaching one is a defect of the\\\\\\\\n   caller, and it stays a host failure, never a guest exception\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\").\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRestoring the strict bounds closes this by construction: every admitted count is < 2^63 in\\\\\\\\nmagnitude, so it is at most 63 bits and every acquisition result at most ~64 bits — 64 is below\\\\\\\\nany `::max-bits` this profile can be registered under, so `integer/add`/`integer/sub` can never\\\\\\\\nreach `out`'s limit branch on the ownership path, and `big!` can never see a non-integer because\\\\\\\\nadmission already proved the carrier. The admitted domain is then a strict subset of the domain\\\\\\\\nthe arithmetic is total over, which is what \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"match admitted count carriers and all ordinary\\\\\\\\nownership operations to one safe arithmetic domain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" has to mean; the unbounded version matched\\\\\\\\nthem to a domain where the arithmetic is only *conditionally* total.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**This is reasoning from source, not a reproduction.** I could not run it. It is falsifiable in\\\\\\\\none command by the next seat (§6, step 1): set `:rc` to an exact integer wider than the\\\\\\\\ncomposition's `::integer/max-bits` (100000 in these lanes, 4096 at the profile floor) at the\\\\\\\\nhandover's admission and watch the second ownership operation refuse.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 4. Why the bounds also settle the rule-1 / rule-3 question (and the dispatch's fourth bullet)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`docs/design/yang.antlr.md` :3868-3876:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n> 1. A payload valid as an exception in its own runtime retains that runtime's ordinary\\\\\\\\n>    catching and identity behavior.\\\\\\\\n> 3. A runtime requiring exception objects, such as Python or PHP, represents an otherwise\\\\\\\\n>    foreign payload with its own `ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and\\\\\\\\n>    original payload.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nr9 read rule 1 as making the endpoints local (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a handle whose count is the native maximum … is\\\\\\\\nVALID local state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"). The dispatch reads the other way, and so does the code: it requires\\\\\\\\nMAX-1 and 2^53 to be pinned *safe through an ordinary catch/return* and separately requires\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the r7 boundary controls stay green\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" — and r7's boundary controls assert that the two\\\\\\\\nendpoints are foreign. Those two requirements are only simultaneously satisfiable with the\\\\\\\\nbounds kept, because reception's acquisition is what moves MAX-1 *onto* MAX: the endpoint is\\\\\\\\nexcluded at admission, the count just below it is admitted and then leaves the range safely\\\\\\\\nunder exact arithmetic. That is also the honest reading of rule 1/rule 3 — a count at the exact\\\\\\\\nendpoint of php's own int encoding is not state php's machinery could have produced and still\\\\\\\\noperate on inside that encoding, so it is \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"otherwise foreign\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and is retained whole (rule 3),\\\\\\\\nwhile every count php *could* have produced keeps its identity through unlimited acquisitions\\\\\\\\n(rule 1). No broad host catch was added; nothing is caught after the fact.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 5. Pin map — each required state, and where it is pinned\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Required state (dispatch) | Where | Observations |\\\\\\\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\\\\\\\n| `:rc` = MAX-1 through an ordinary catch/return stays SAFE (no overflow) | `php-count-acquisition-test`, shape `near-max` | `-returned` = route `:return` (at the pre-fix tip this leg dies in `lower-return`'s acquisition with `Math.addExact` \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"long overflow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"); `-identical` = `(= payload value)`, payload identity preserved |\\\\\\\\n| `:rc` = 9007199254740992 (JS BigInt boundary; JVM-verifiable as a number, Node reasoning noted) | same test, shape `js-number-boundary` | same two observations. The reasoning is recorded in `count-source`'s docstring: a plain Long in this JVM lane (where it is verified), a BigInt carrier on JS where the same decimal parses past the safe-Number range and a raw `(+ rc 1)` throws `TypeError: Cannot mix BigInt and other types` (sol's Node primitive diagnostic, both signs). The tri-host acceptance lane covers Node; **this laptop lane cannot and does not claim to.** |\\\\\\\\n| `:rc` = `(/ 1 2)` admitted as FOREIGN with full retention map | `php-handle-shaped-foreign-payload-test`, shape `ratio-count` | all four per-shape observations: `-retained` (route `:return`, the wrapper came back), `-local` (`is_foreign` on the wrapper), `-condition` (`later` reads `$saved` and compares the WHOLE condition map, `:yang.call/data {:complete [:evidence]}` included), `-payload` (original payload identity inside the retained condition) |\\\\\\\\n| the r7 boundary controls stay green | same test, shapes `max-long-count`, `min-long-count` — restored | same four observations each |\\\\\\\\n| missing / non-number / legitimate-local controls kept | `string-class-no-rc`, `non-number-count`, `authentic-cell-bad-class` (`:rc 1`, re-check3's coverage note), `php-local-reception-directed-test`, plus the two new local count shapes | unchanged |\\\\\\\\n| kit parity + existing pin set | untouched tests | **NOT RUN** |\\\\\\\\n| 18 lane-blocked assertions byte-identical | `php-kit-batch-parity-test` and the fixtures were not edited (T8) | **not re-observed** — no shell |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTwelve shapes × 4 observations = a 48-key expected map per backend (`:semantic`, `:stack`,\\\\\\\\n`:register`), computed from the shape list, all in ONE composed program.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDeliberately NOT added, disclosed: a **float-count** foreign shape. re-check4 says \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Keep the\\\\\\\\nmissing/non-number, float, boundary and legitimate local controls\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", but no float-count shape\\\\\\\\nexists at 265c4a45 to keep (r7's eleven are enumerated in r7's findings), and every way of\\\\\\\\nencoding one in the observation language is either host-divergent (a bare integral `1.0`: JS\\\\\\\\ncannot distinguish it from `1`, which is why the prelude builds floats with `data/float64`) or\\\\\\\\nunverifiable from here (whether a `1.5` literal / `data/float64` application survives the kit's\\\\\\\\nobservation-form emission). Because all twelve shapes share ONE composed program and ONE\\\\\\\\nexpected map, a single mis-emitting shape would red the restored r7 controls along with it — an\\\\\\\\nunacceptable bet with no way to run it. The exact-integer predicate already rejects every\\\\\\\\nnon-integral carrier by construction, and `ratio-count` pins the case that fooled both\\\\\\\\n`data/number?` and `php/int?`. **Suggested for the next seat, which can run it:** add\\\\\\\\n`[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"float-count\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" …]` with a `data/float64`-built non-integral count and confirm all four\\\\\\\\nobservations.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 6. What the next seat must run (in this order), and the exact commit\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nNothing below was executed here. Each command foreground, explicit timeout, nothing backgrounded,\\\\\\\\nfocused namespaces only; `yang.call.exception-contract-test` NEVER locally (shiva-only).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\ncd /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-phpexc\\\\\\\\ngit status --porcelain          # expect exactly: M prelude.cljc, M php_exception_test.clj (+ untracked collab/)\\\\\\\\ngit diff --stat HEAD            # sanity: two files\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# 1. RED-FIRST for §3 (do this before anything else; it is the only new claim needing proof).\\\\\\\\n#    At the HANDOVER admission (integer/integer? alone) build a :rc wider than the composition's\\\\\\\\n#    ::integer/max-bits, e.g. by squaring the long maximum with the VM's raw host `*` (six\\\\\\\\n#    squarings clear the 4096 profile floor, eleven clear these lanes' 100000), and drive it\\\\\\\\n#    through reception plus one ordinary acquisition. Expect: the count becomes\\\\\\\\n#    :yin.vm.integer/bit-limit and the next ownership op throws \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"integer primitive refused\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n#    {:reason :wrong-type, :op sub}.\\\\\\\\n#    Then confirm the same payload under THIS tree is foreign with complete retention.\\\\\\\\n# 2. RED-FIRST for the two residuals at 265c4a45 (sol's counterexamples), so the chronology is\\\\\\\\n#    real: MAX-1 through catch/return -> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"long overflow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; (/ 1 2) -> integer/compare :wrong-type.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\ntimeout 900 clojure -M:test -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n '(do (require (quote [clojure.test :as t])) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      (require (quote yang.call.php-exception-test)) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      (t/run-tests (quote yang.call.php-exception-test)))'\\\\\\\\n# the twelve focused namespaces r7 used, at minimum:\\\\\\\\n#   yang.call.php-exception-test, yang.php.antlr.prelude-test, yang.php.antlr.class-model-test,\\\\\\\\n#   yang.php.antlr.objects-test, yang.php.antlr.generators-test,\\\\\\\\n#   yang.php.antlr.builtin-surfaces-test, yang.php.antlr.e2e-fast-test,\\\\\\\\n#   yang.php.antlr.exception-source-test, dao.host.conformance-test, yin.vm.integer-test\\\\\\\\n# yang.php.antlr.prelude-test is the one that guards P8 (the host-names change): its four static\\\\\\\\n# pins read that list. If any of them goes red, restore the `data/number?` token and re-run — the\\\\\\\\n# rest of this round does not depend on that cleanup.\\\\\\\\ntimeout 300 clojure -M:clj-kondo --lint src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                                        test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj   # expect 0/0\\\\\\\\ntimeout 300 clojure -M:cljstyle check src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n                                      test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj     # expect clean\\\\\\\\ngit add src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc test/yang/call/php_exception_test.clj \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        collab/exception-track/1794518915000-engineer-php-r1r2r3-r10.qwen3.8-max.findings.md\\\\\\\\ngit commit -m \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fix(yang.php): the count domain is the engine's exact integer arithmetic end to end (r10, sol's re-check4 completion)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n# DO NOT PUSH — the orchestrator pushes after review coordination.\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIf `cljstyle check` reports anything, run `cljstyle fix` on the two files and re-run the probes.\\\\\\\\nThe formatting risk is small because almost nothing here is newly hand-formatted: `php/obj-val?`\\\\\\\\nkeeps HEAD's own nesting and indentation (one predicate level collapsed out of it), the two\\\\\\\\nrestored shapes are HEAD's bytes, and the only reflowed line is r9's wrapped `cell/set!` in\\\\\\\\n`php/addref` (its continuations align to `assoc`'s first argument and to `do`'s body column, which\\\\\\\\nis what cljstyle would produce). Nothing was written to either file except through targeted\\\\\\\\nedits, so no whole-file reformat landed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTo diff the working tree against HEAD without a shell of your own, `/private/tmp/phpexc-review`\\\\\\\\nstill holds a detached checkout of `265c4a45` (if it has not been cleaned up): reading the same\\\\\\\\npath in both trees gives the exact delta. `git diff HEAD` in this worktree is authoritative once\\\\\\\\nyou have a shell.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 7. Open questions I had to decide alone (`ask_user_question` is unavailable in this mode)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **Endpoints: local or foreign?** Decided FOREIGN (bounds restored) — §4. This is the one\\\\\\\\n   judgement call that reverses a handover hunk, and it is the one the orchestrator should look\\\\\\\\n   at first. If the owner/architect rules that rule 1 makes the endpoints valid local state, the\\\\\\\\n   revert is small: drop the two `integer/compare` clauses in `php/obj-val?`, move\\\\\\\\n   `max-long-count`/`min-long-count` back into `php-count-acquisition-test`, and accept §3's\\\\\\\\n   `::bit-limit` escape (which would then need a different closure — e.g. refusing a count whose\\\\\\\\n   `integer/add` answers a keyword, which is a check on the result rather than the domain).\\\\\\\\n2. **`php/finalize!`'s raw `(- rc 1)`**: left alone, with the safety argument recorded in §2.\\\\\\\\n3. **float-count shape**: not added, §5.\\\\\\\\n4. **Commit**: not made. Leaving the tree dirty is deliberate — a commit I cannot gate would put\\\\\\\\n   an unverified r10 in history under a message claiming a completion I did not observe. The next\\\\\\\\n   seat can `git add`/`commit` in one step once §6's gates are green.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 8. Residuals and carry-overs (unchanged, not re-litigated here)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSteward-owned: the §3a instrument/lane conflict (the lane-blocked fixtures), the dao.jing\\\\\\\\nlaptop-lane stall, the lifecycle instrument preference, suspension/context-lifetime obligations,\\\\\\\\nand the remote/foundation merge gates — the contract test, `test:changed`, the 12-pair matrix and\\\\\\\\nthe Node/Dart harnesses. re-check4's coverage note on the committed nil pin (it checks retain +\\\\\\\\n`is_foreign == false` but not the exact TypeError class or provenance absence) still stands:\\\\\\\\n`php-nil-condition-reception-test` was not extended in this round, because extending it is a new\\\\\\\\npin I cannot run. Per project memory, this round's local green — had there been any — would not\\\\\\\\nhave moved ledger rows: acceptance is the shiva-only contract kit deciding at the PUBLIC entries,\\\\\\\\nand everything above is a different instrument.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}}\\\\n9:{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item_3\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rg -n '8.10.7|rule 3|EXACT-INTEGER|endpoint|bounds' docs/design/datom.world.abi.md; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*recheck*findings*' -g '*r9*findings*' -g '*r8*findings*'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"---\\\\\\\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# BUILD & TEST\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Commands\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\\\\\\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\\\\\\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\\\\\\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\\\\\\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\\\\\\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\\\\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\\\\\\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\\\\\\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\\\\\\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\\\\\\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\\\\\\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\\\\\\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\\\\\\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\\\\\\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\\\\\\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\\\\\\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\\\\\\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\\\\\\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" below for\\\\\\\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\\\\\\\n#\\\\\\\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\\\\\\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\\\\\\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\\\\\\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\\\\\\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\\\\\\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\\\\\\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\\\\\\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\\\\\\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\\\\\\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\\\\\\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\\\\\\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\\\\\\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\\\\\\\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\\\\\\\\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\\\\\\\\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\\\\\\\\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\\\\\\\\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\\\\\\\\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\\\\\\\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\\\\\\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\\\\\\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\\\\\\\\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01;\\\\\\\\n# for a fix branch, the previously-failing tests green IS the merge bar\\\\\\\\n# (owner ruling 2026-11-07; see Gate Ladder G0).\\\\\\\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\\\\\\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\\\\\\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\\\\\\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" below).\\\\\\\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\\\\\\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\\\\\\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\\\\\\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\\\\\\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\\\\\\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\\\\\\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\\\\\\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\\\\\\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\\\\\\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\\\\\\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\\\\\\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\\\\\\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\\\\\\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\\\\\\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\\\\\\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\\\\\\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\\\\\\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\\\\\\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\\\\\\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\\\\\\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\\\\\\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\\\\\\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\\\\\\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\\\\\\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\\\\\\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\\\\\\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\\\\\\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\\\\\\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\\\\\\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\\\\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\\\\\\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\\\\\\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\\\\\\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\\\\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\\\\\\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\\\\\\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\\\\\\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\\\\\\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\\\\\\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\\\\\\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\\\\\\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\\\\\\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\\\\\\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\\\\\\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\\\\\\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\\\\\\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\\\\\\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\\\\\\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\\\\\\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\\\\\\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\\\\\\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\\\\\\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\\\\\\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\\\\\\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\\\\\\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\\\\\\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\\\\\\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\\\\\\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\\\\\\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\\\\\\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\\\\\\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\\\\\\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\\\\\\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\\\\\\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\\\\\\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\\\\\\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\\\\\\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\\\\\\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\\\\\\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\\\\\\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\\\\\\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\\\\\\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\\\\\\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\\\\\\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\\\\\\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\\\\\\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\\\\\\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\\\\\\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\\\\\\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\\\\\\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\\\\\\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\\\\\\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\\\\\\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\\\\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\\\\\\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\\\\\\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\\\\\\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\\\\\\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\\\\\\\navailable. **Individual tests may run on the laptop; suite-scale runs may\\\\\\\\nnot (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\\\\\\\\nreproduces quickly and is fine locally. Everything suite-scale — subsystem\\\\\\\\nlane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\\\\\\\\n`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\\\\\\\\noutgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\\\\\\\\nprocesses each multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core\\\\\\\\ncount); four of them GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over\\\\\\\\n100 on 2026-11-01 while making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier:\\\\\\\\njupiter 5,645 s at 12 workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane\\\\\\\\nset concurrently on the laptop either.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\\\\\\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\\\\\\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\\\\\\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\\\\\\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\\\\\\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\\\\\\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\\\\\\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\\\\\\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\\\\\\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\\\\\\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\\\\\\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\\\\\\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\\\\\\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\\\\\\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\\\\\\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\\\\\\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\\\\\\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\\\\\\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\\\\\\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\\\\\\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\\\\\\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\\\\\\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\\\\\\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\\\\\\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\\\\\\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\\\\\\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\\\\\\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\\\\\\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\\\\\\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\\\\\\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\\\\\\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\\\\\\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\\\\\\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\\\\\\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\\\\\\\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\\\\\\\\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\\\\\\\\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\\\\\\\\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\\\\\\\\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\\\\\\\\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>[/<test-name>]` (owner ruling\\\\\\\\n   2026-11-07: the focus rule runs the failing TEST, not the namespace —\\\\\\\\n   `NS/test-name` entries become flutter `--plain-name` filters so only the\\\\\\\\n   named deftests execute; fall back to bare `<ns>` only when the failing\\\\\\\\n   var inside the namespace is not yet known, and narrow as soon as it is).\\\\\\\\n   Fix against the failing test alone, re-run it green, then climb back up\\\\\\\\n   (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\\\\\\\\n   Dart bug-chases may set `DATOM_FAIL_FAST=1` (owner ruling 2026-11-07): the\\\\\\\\n   scoped run stops at the first failure so the report arrives in minutes instead\\\\\\\\n   of after the full scoped set. Gate runs never set it — the merge bar needs the\\\\\\\\n   complete pass/fail picture. All dart runs use `--reporter expanded`, which\\\\\\\\n   streams failure names and positions inline so an interrupted run still retains\\\\\\\\n   them (the nightly's compact reporter lost 7 failure names to its 30000s wall,\\\\\\\\n   2026-11-06).\\\\\\\\n   Merge bar for a fix branch (owner ruling 2026-11-07, sharpening the 2026-11-01\\\\\\\\n   ruling): when the work is fixing already-failing tests, the previously-failing\\\\\\\\n   tests re-run green IS the merge bar — a fix branch merges on that alone; the\\\\\\\\n   G1/G2 climb applies to new feature slices, not to fixes. The full suite still\\\\\\\\n   gets its end-to-end confirmation from the daily nightly (04:00 VN), which is\\\\\\\\n   what catches collateral damage, not a pre-merge suite run.\\\\\\\\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\\\\\\\\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\\\\\\\\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\\\\\\\\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\\\\\\\\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\\\\\\\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\\\\\\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\\\\\\\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\\\\\\\\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\\\\\\\\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\\\\\\\\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\\\\\\\\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Cadence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" below), so a\\\\\\\\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\\\\\\\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\\\\\\\\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\\\\\\\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\\\\\\\\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\\\\\\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\\\\\\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\\\\\\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\\\\\\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\\\\\\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\\\\\\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\\\\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\\\\\\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\\\\\\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\\\\\\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\\\\\\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\\\\\\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\\\\\\\n  REPL.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\\\\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\\\\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\\\\\\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# ClojureDart\\\\\\\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# Linting\\\\\\\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\\\\\\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\\\\\\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\\\\\\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\\\\\\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\\\\\\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\\\\\\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\\\\\\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\\\\\\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\\\\\\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\\\\\\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\\\\\\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\\\\\\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\\\\\\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\\\\\\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\\\\\\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\\\\\\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\\\\\\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\\\\\\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\\\\\\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\\\\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\\\\\\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\\\\\\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\\\\\\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\\\\\\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"): a deftest that\\\\\\\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\\\\\\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\\\\\\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\\\\\\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\\\\\\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\\\\\\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\\\\\\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\\\\\\\n- **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-05):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\\\\\\\\n  feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\\\\\\\\n  `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\\\\\\\\n  including the slow tier, runs **once a day at 04:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** as a\\\\\\\\n  disjoint lane pair (`src/dev/nightly.clj`): **jupiter takes the jvm lane, shiva\\\\\\\\n  takes node and dart** (each lane in its own clone; shiva publishes the run pin,\\\\\\\\n  jupiter reads it and falls back to its own current master as an advisory run).\\\\\\\\n  The measured long set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be\\\\\\\\n  a per-merge cost; a break is instead caught by the next scheduled run.\\\\\\\\n  The schedule is an **hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard** in\\\\\\\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on each host (the guard fires only at the 04 VN hour and\\\\\\\\n  `flock` skips a slot whose previous run is still active), so it is immune to the\\\\\\\\n  DST shifts of either host's local timezone. **The cron entries are currently\\\\\\\\n  REMOVED (owner instruction 2026-11-06)**: the wrappers remain installed on both\\\\\\\\n  hosts and re-arming is one crontab line each\\\\\\\\n  (`0 * * * * ~/ci/nightly-cron.sh >> ~/ci/nightly-cron.log 2>&1`). Logs:\\\\\\\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.log`; results: `~/ci/nightly-results/<stamp>/summary.txt`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Remote test\\\\\\\\nservers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" for how to run them):\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Run | Time |\\\\\\\\n|---|---|\\\\\\\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\\\\\\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\\\\\\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\\\\\\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\\\\\\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\\\\\\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\\\\\\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\\\\\\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\\\\\\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\\\\\\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\\\\\\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\\\\\\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\\\\\\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\\\\\\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\\\\\\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\\\\\\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\\\\\\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\\\\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\\\\\\\nset -u\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ncd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${2:-8}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nSUBS=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n: > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\\\\\\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$s\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\");print}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  cat \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ndone\\\\\\\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\\\\\\\nfind test -type f \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\( -name \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"*_test.clj\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -o -name \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"*_test.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\) | sed -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#^test/##\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.cljc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\$##\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#/#.#g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -e \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"s#_#-#g\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | sort -u > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nsort -u \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ncomm -23 \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrun_shard() {\\\\\\\\n  s=$1; f=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; [ -s \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" ] || return 0\\\\\\\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"); done < \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$f\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${args[@]}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\\\\\\\nprintf \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"%s\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"run_shard {}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\\\\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\\\\\\\nset -u\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ncd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\\\\\nPAR=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${2:-6}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nOUT=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; rm -rf \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nrun_ns() {\\\\\\\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\\\\\\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\\\\\n  echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"^Ran \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tail -1)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" > \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nxargs -P \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$PAR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"run_ns {}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" < \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >> \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\\\\\\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\\\\\\\nand `git`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\\\\\\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). mise was moved off the apt package to the official user-local install (`~/.local/bin/mise`, 2026.11.2, owner action 2026-11-07) so both hosts share shiva's layout; toolchains in `~/.local/share/mise/installs/` were untouched. Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\\\\\\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\\\\\\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Kill remote processes by exact PID, never by name pattern\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOn these hosts, kill a runaway nightly/lane process by its exact PID\\\\\\\\n(`pgrep -af <pattern>` only to LIST, then `kill <pid>`), never with\\\\\\\\n`pkill -f <string>` / `killall <name>`. Measured 2026-11-06: `pkill -f\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"nightly.clj\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` over SSH matched the invoking bash command line itself\\\\\\\\n(the pattern appears in `ps` output of the session carrying the kill),\\\\\\\\nterminating the SSH session mid-command — exit 255 with the kill\\\\\\\\nhalf-done. A name-pattern kill also races concurrent seats: another\\\\\\\\norchestrator's delegate process whose arguments embed the same string\\\\\\\\ndies with yours. If the PID from `pgrep -af` is ambiguous (several\\\\\\\\ncandidates), inspect with `ps -o pid,etime,args -p <pid>` first and kill\\\\\\\\nthe exact process; a session that dies under you is a symptom of this\\\\\\\\nmistake, not a host outage.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\\\\\\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\\\\\\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\\\\\\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\\\\\\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\\\\\\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\\\\\\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\\\\\\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\\\\\\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$URL\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\";\\\\\\\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\\\\\\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\\\\\\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\\\\\\\\nfrom — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\\\\\\\\nrefs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\\\\\\\\nscratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\\\\\\\\nevery lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\\\\\\\\n`checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\\\\\\\\nmaster, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\\\\\\\\ntree that did not contain them.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nBefore any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\\\\\\\ngit remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\\\\\\\\ngit fetch -q origin \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ngit checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\\\\\\\\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIf `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$URL\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`). If\\\\\\\\nthe `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\\\\\\\\nand recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\\\\\\\\nreports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\\\\\\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\\\\\\\ninto the clone:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\\\\\\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\\\\\\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\\\\\\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\\\\\\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Can't\\\\\\\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"One lane set at a time\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" are per-machine rules about\\\\\\\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\\\\\\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"on one machine where\\\\\\\\navoidable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\\\\\\\\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\\\\\\\\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\\\\\\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\\\\\\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\\\\\\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\\\\\\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\\\\\\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\\\\\\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\\\\\\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"), not yet\\\\\\\\n  verified by a measured run.\\\\\\\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\\\\\\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\\\\\\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\\\\\\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\\\\\\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\\\\\\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\\\\\\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\\\\\\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\\\\\\\nseparate Dart clone.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n**The nightly driver now enforces this by construction.** `src/dev/nightly.clj`\\\\\\\\n(2026-11-03, kimi-reviewed) runs a multi-lane invocation as one concurrent\\\\\\\\nfuture per lane, each in its OWN clone — jvm keeps the primary clone, node\\\\\\\\nand dart get `<primary>-node` / `<primary>-dart`. The pinned SHA is resolved\\\\\\\\nonce and checked out in every lane clone; a cache-warm pass\\\\\\\\n(`clojure -P -M:test:cljs:clojuredart:cljd`) runs in the primary before\\\\\\\\nfan-out so two clones never fetch into the shared `~/.m2`/`~/.gitlibs`\\\\\\\\nconcurrently; per-lane summaries are appended serially after the futures\\\\\\\\njoin. A single-lane invocation (jupiter's `jvm`) is unchanged: one clone,\\\\\\\\none serial lane. Both hosts run the same driver — the lane list on the\\\\\\\\ncommand line (shiva: `node,dart` + `--pin-publish`; jupiter: `jvm` +\\\\\\\\n`--pin-read --pin-peer shiva...`) is the only difference.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\\\\\\\\nitself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\\\\\\\\nfirst lane:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\\\\\\\\n   the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\\\\\\\\n   node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\\\\\\\\n2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\\\\\\\\n   `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\\\\\\\\n   only (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\\\\\\\\n   parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\\\\\\\\n   `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\\\\\\\\n   `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\\\\\\\\n   the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\\\\\\\\n   (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\\\\\\\\n   (2026-11-02). The lane-runner fix landed 2026-11-03 (eb27fd47).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Merge verification: run the suite from a fresh clone, not the authoring worktree\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA merge whose new code reads a file that was never `git add`ed passes in\\\\\\\\nthe authoring worktree (the untracked file is still on disk there) and\\\\\\\\nbreaks everywhere else. Measured: the H3-0 landing (1fd7236b) merged\\\\\\\\n`kernel-declarations` — which reads\\\\\\\\n`yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn` via `io/resource` at load —\\\\\\\\nwithout the file (untracked in the authoring worktree); master's\\\\\\\\nyang.latte-oracle-test died at load until the file was restored\\\\\\\\n(2026-11-04). Before landing any merge that adds code reading a new\\\\\\\\nresource, or after any merge at all when in doubt:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\ngit worktree add /tmp/merge-verify <merge-commit>\\\\\\\\ncd /tmp/merge-verify && <the touched subsystem's focused test>\\\\\\\\ngit worktree remove /tmp/merge-verify\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA fresh clone/worktree has only committed files — a missing untracked\\\\\\\\nresource fails there immediately.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\\\\\\\\n`orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\\\\\\\\nand merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\\\\\\\\nand a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\\\\\\\\n(measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\\\\\\\\n`<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\\\\\\\\nkeep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\\\\\\\\nchange to a ledger file:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\ngit diff --cached | grep -E '^\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\\\\\\\\ngit grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIf either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\\\\\\\\na ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\\\\\\\\nlists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\\\\\\\\nstaging.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\\\\\\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\\\\\\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\\\\\\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\\\\\\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\\\\\\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\\\\\\\n5-6-hour run.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\\\\\\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\\\\\\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\\\\\\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\\\\\\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\\\\\\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\\\\\\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\\\\\\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\\\\\\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\\\\\\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\\\\\\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\\\\\\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\\\\\\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\\\\\\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\\\\\\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\\\\\\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\\\\\\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\\\\\\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\\\\\\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### The full run\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\\\\\\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\\\\\\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\\\\\\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\\\\\\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\\\\\\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\\\\\\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\\\\\\\nset -u\\\\\\\\nexport PATH=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nDART=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"${2:-}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\ncd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" || exit 2\\\\\\\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\\\\\\\neval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nLOG=\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$1/target-ci\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; mkdir -p \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$@\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\\\\\\\n         echo \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; }\\\\\\\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\\\\\\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\\\\\\\nif [ -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\\\\\\\n  ( cd \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$DART\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 2>&1\\\\\\\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\\\\\\\nfi\\\\\\\\nwait\\\\\\\\necho \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"done $(ts)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" | tee -a \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```sh\\\\\\\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\\\\\\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\\\\\\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\\\\\\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\\\\\\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\\\\\\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\\\\\\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\\\\\\\\n  MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"a second UDP bind on\\\\\\\\n  a taken port behaves differently on Linux\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\\\\\\\\n  `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\\\\\\\\n  `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\\\\\\\\n  holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\\\\\\\\n  kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\\\\\\\\n  throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\\\\\\\\n  `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\\\\\\\\n  rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\\\\\\\\n  (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"127.0.0.1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\\\\\\\\n  points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\\\\\\\\n  on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\\\\\\\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\\\\\\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\\\\\\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\\\\\\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n### The nightly full suite and the branc\n... 182555 bytes omitted ...\n\\\\\\\"'$refuse_BANG_'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nyin.vm.integer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'$wrong_type_BANG_'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nyin.vm.integer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'$big_BANG_'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nyin.vm.integer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'$int_compare'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'```'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNo local ForeignException or retained condition was delivered. Float64 and bare\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDouble controls were rejected cleanly with complete retention; an integral\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndivision yielding the native integer 2 remained local.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRequired completion: establish the actual integer primitive argument domain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbefore calling integer/compare, using an exact-integer predicate or equivalent\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntotal validation. php/int? is a PHP representation classifier, not that predicate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nPin the computed rational count as foreign, with complete condition/evidence and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\noriginal payload identity. Keep the missing/non-number, float, boundary and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlegitimate local controls. This is a new refusal introduced by the r7 validation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npath, not an operational failure explicitly requested by the foreign callable.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## r8 ruling — correct shape, correct placement, no new mint surface\\\\\\\\\\\\